# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In previous lessons, you studied the noise that arises in quantum hardware and worked through current techniques for suppressing and mitigating it. This lesson takes the next step along the path from error mitigation to error correction: instead of actively correcting detected errors, we detect them and discard the runs they touched.\n"
msgstr "Sa mga nakaraang aralin, pinag-aralan mo ang noise na lumilitaw sa quantum hardware at ang mga kasalukuyang teknik para sugpuin at bawasan ito. Ang araling ito ang susunod na hakbang sa landas mula error mitigation patungong error correction: sa halip na aktibong itama ang mga natukoy na error, dine-detect natin ang mga ito at itinatapon ang mga run na naapektuhan nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<IBMVideo id=\"135063108\" title=\"In this video, Chris Porter describes methods for detecting quantum errors, including parity checks, basic stabilizer checks, and coherent Pauli checks. The text below provides more detail.\"/>\n"
msgstr "<IBMVideo id=\"135063108\" title=\"Sa video na ito, inilalarawan ni Chris Porter ang mga paraan para mag-detect ng mga quantum error, kasama ang parity check, basic stabilizer check, at coherent Pauli check. Mas detalyado ang teksto sa ibaba.\"/>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We call this **post-selected quantum error correction**. A set of checks is attached to the circuit, a run that fails a check is flagged as containing an error and discarded, and the accepted runs are used as they are. Nothing is decoded and nothing is corrected, so this workflow does not require real-time decoding or recovery; the price is paid in discarded shots instead. Throughout this lesson, error detection means this post-selected form. [Section 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection) is the limiting case with no code at all: the check is a symmetry of the ideal output, read directly from the measured bits. Beginning with [Section 1.2](#stabilizer-code), the checks are stabilizer measurements on ancilla qubits, first the $[[4,2,2]]$ error-detecting code at the end of the circuit, then coherent Pauli checks and spacetime checks placed around and inside the payload.\n"
msgstr "Tinatawag natin itong **post-selected quantum error correction**. Isang set ng mga check ang idinadagdag sa circuit, ang run na bumagsak sa isang check ay mamarkahang may error at itatapon, at ang mga tinanggap na run ay gagamitin nang ganoon lang. Walang dine-decode at walang itinatama, kaya hindi kailangan ng workflow na ito ang real-time decoding o recovery; ang kapalit ay ang mga itinapon na shot. Sa buong araling ito, ang error detection ay tumutukoy sa post-selected na anyong ito. Ang [Seksyon 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection) ang limiting case na walang code: ang check ay isang symmetry ng ideal na output, na direktang binabasa mula sa mga nasukat na bit. Simula sa [Seksyon 1.2](#stabilizer-code), ang mga check ay mga stabilizer measurement sa mga ancilla qubit, una ang $[[4,2,2]]$ error-detecting code sa dulo ng circuit, saka ang mga coherent Pauli check at spacetime check na nakalagay sa paligid at loob ng payload.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum error mitigation and quantum error correction (QEC) are often framed as tools for different eras of quantum computing: mitigation for today's devices and correction for scalable fault tolerance. IBM&reg; instead describes [a continuous path from error mitigation to error correction](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum), along which removing errors trades time (samples) against space (qubits). At one end, we have error suppression and mitigation. Some of these techniques are low-overhead. But others, like Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC), pay a sampling cost that grows quickly, even exponentially with the noise in the system. At the other end, full quantum error correction can make a computation reliable in a single shot, but large-scale fault-tolerant architectures require substantial qubit and gate overhead; their connectivity, error-rate, and decoding requirements depend on the code and architecture. Post-selected error correction sits between them, and spacetime codes can make it hardware-efficient by distributing low-weight Pauli checks across both space and time in a circuit. The [Qiskit Paulice](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qiskit-paulice) addon lets you place such checks in your own circuits today (see its [documentation](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-paulice)). In [this blog post](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum), IBM reports that a recent demonstration encoded 64 logical qubits in spacetime codes on 76 physical qubits, in a circuit that also contained 314 $T$ gates, and gave roughly a tenfold effective improvement in gate error rates after post-selection.\n"
msgstr "Madalas na ituring ang quantum error mitigation at quantum error correction (QEC) bilang mga kasangkapan para sa magkaibang panahon ng quantum computing: mitigation para sa mga device ngayon at correction para sa scalable na fault tolerance. Sa halip, inilalarawan ng IBM&reg; ang [tuloy-tuloy na landas mula error mitigation patungong error correction](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum), kung saan ang pag-alis ng mga error ay nagpapalit ng oras (mga sample) laban sa espasyo (mga qubit). Sa isang dulo, nariyan ang error suppression at mitigation. Mababa ang overhead ng ilan sa mga teknik na ito. Pero ang iba, tulad ng Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC), ay may sampling cost na mabilis lumaki, kahit exponential pa, kasabay ng noise sa sistema. Sa kabilang dulo, magagawang maaasahan ng buong quantum error correction ang isang computation sa isang shot lang, pero nangangailangan ang malalaking fault-tolerant architecture ng malaking qubit at gate overhead; nakadepende sa code at architecture ang mga kinakailangan nila sa connectivity, error rate, at decoding. Nasa pagitan nila ang post-selected error correction, at magagawa itong hardware-efficient ng mga spacetime code sa pamamagitan ng pamamahagi ng mga low-weight Pauli check sa espasyo at oras ng circuit. Hinahayaan ka ng [Qiskit Paulice](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qiskit-paulice) addon na maglagay ng mga ganitong check sa sarili mong mga circuit ngayon (tingnan ang [dokumentasyon](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-paulice) nito). Sa [blog post na ito](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum), iniulat ng IBM na ang isang kamakailang demonstration ay nag-encode ng 64 logical qubit sa mga spacetime code sa 76 physical qubit, sa isang circuit na may 314 ding $T$ gate, at nagbigay ng humigit-kumulang sampung ulit na epektibong pagbuti sa gate error rate pagkatapos ng post-selection.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The basic protocol is as follows. One first selects a detection strategy. Depending on the strategy, the original circuit is either augmented with additional gates that implement an explicit consistency check (placed internally in the circuit, or at the end), or, when the ideal output obeys a known constraint, the circuit is run unchanged and that constraint serves as the check. The (augmented) circuit is then executed and measured, and the check outcomes, or the structural constraint evaluated on the measured bitstrings, are used to flag shots as likely corrupted and reject them in classical post-processing (or recover the correct state probabilistically, as in configuration recovery). The aim is to improve the accepted samples, at the cost of fewer usable shots, and the benefit must be checked against the errors introduced by the detection circuit.\n"
msgstr "Ganito ang pangunahing protocol. Una, pipili ng detection strategy. Depende sa strategy, ang orihinal na circuit ay dinadagdagan ng mga gate na nagpapatupad ng tahasang consistency check (inilalagay sa loob ng circuit, o sa dulo), o, kapag ang ideal na output ay sumusunod sa kilalang constraint, pinapatakbo ang circuit nang walang pagbabago at ang constraint na iyon ang nagsisilbing check. Pagkatapos ay isinasagawa at sinusukat ang (dinagdagang) circuit, at ginagamit ang mga resulta ng check, o ang structural constraint na sinuri sa mga nasukat na bitstring, para markahan ang mga shot bilang malamang na sira at tanggihan ang mga ito sa classical post-processing (o mabawi nang probabilistic ang tamang state, tulad ng sa configuration recovery). Layunin nitong pagandahin ang mga tinanggap na sample, kapalit ng mas kaunting magagamit na shot, at dapat suriin ang benepisyo laban sa mga error na dulot ng detection circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson builds up to spacetime codes through a continuum of post-selection techniques, starting from the simplest &mdash; a symmetry rule on the measured bitstring &mdash; and ending with checks whose detection regions reach deep into the circuit's interior. In the hardware examples in Parts 1 and 2, we weigh the gain from post-selection against its cost in added gates and discarded shots.\n"
msgstr "Binubuo ng araling ito ang daan patungo sa mga spacetime code sa pamamagitan ng sunud-sunod na mga post-selection technique, simula sa pinakasimple &mdash; isang symmetry rule sa nasukat na bitstring &mdash; at nagtatapos sa mga check na ang detection region ay umaabot nang malalim sa loob ng circuit. Sa mga hardware example sa Part 1 at 2, tinitimbang natin ang pakinabang ng post-selection laban sa gastos nito sa mga dagdag na gate at itinapon na shot.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson is organized in two parts.\n"
msgstr "Nahahati sa dalawang bahagi ang araling ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 1: End-of-circuit detection. All check information is read out at the end of the circuit. We consider two strategies under this constraint: in [Section 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection), the circuit is run unchanged and a known symmetry of the ideal output distribution serves as the check; in [Section 1.2](#stabilizer-code), additional gates are appended at the end of the circuit, and their measurement outcome serves as the check.\n"
msgstr "Part 1: End-of-circuit detection. Binabasa sa dulo ng circuit ang lahat ng impormasyon ng check. Dalawang strategy ang isinasaalang-alang natin sa ilalim ng limitasyong ito: sa [Seksyon 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection), pinapatakbo ang circuit nang walang pagbabago at ang kilalang symmetry ng ideal na output distribution ang nagsisilbing check; sa [Seksyon 1.2](#stabilizer-code), nagdaragdag ng mga gate sa dulo ng circuit, at ang resulta ng measurement ng mga ito ang nagsisilbing check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 2: Mid-circuit detection. Check operations are sensitive to errors that occur during the circuit's execution, defining a detection region, the set of locations at which a fault would be flagged by the check. We develop two such methods: coherent Pauli checks, which use a paired check construction applicable to any Clifford circuit, and spacetime codes, which exploit the specific circuit's structure to reduce overhead.\n"
msgstr "Part 2: Mid-circuit detection. Sensitibo ang mga check operation sa mga error na nangyayari habang isinasagawa ang circuit, na bumubuo ng detection region, ang set ng mga lokasyon kung saan mamarkahan ng check ang isang fault. Bumubuo tayo ng dalawang ganitong paraan: ang coherent Pauli check, na gumagamit ng paired check construction na naaangkop sa anumang Clifford circuit, at ang spacetime code, na gumagamit sa partikular na estruktura ng circuit para bawasan ang overhead.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fault tolerance remains the north star. The first IBM fault-tolerant quantum computer, IBM Quantum&reg; Starling, is designed around quantum LDPC codes rather than the detection schemes practiced here, and IBM expects error mitigation and post-selection to keep working alongside error correction even then. The skills in this lesson, choosing what to check, placing checks where they catch the most, and accounting for their cost, carry over to that setting.\n"
msgstr "Nananatiling gabay ang fault tolerance. Ang unang fault-tolerant quantum computer ng IBM, ang IBM Quantum&reg; Starling, ay dinisenyo sa paligid ng mga quantum LDPC code sa halip na ng mga detection scheme na sinasanay dito, at inaasahan ng IBM na patuloy na gagana ang error mitigation at post-selection kasabay ng error correction kahit pa noon. Ang mga kasanayan sa araling ito, ang pagpili kung ano ang susuriin, paglalagay ng mga check kung saan pinakamarami ang nahuhuli ng mga ito, at pagtutuos ng gastos ng mga ito, ay naililipat sa ganoong setting.\n"

