# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1 Symmetry post-selection on classical bitstrings {#11-symmetry-post-selection-on-classical-bitstrings}"
msgstr "1.1 Symmetry post-selection sa mga classical bitstring {#11-symmetry-post-selection-on-classical-bitstrings}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parity checking is a classical error-detection technique. The [parity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parity_bit) of a bitstring is the number of `1`s it contains, modulo 2. In the example below, a sender appends a parity bit to each data block so that the total number of `1`s in the resulting codeword is even.\n"
msgstr "Ang parity checking ay isang klasikal na teknik sa pag-detect ng error. Ang [parity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parity_bit) ng isang bitstring ay ang bilang ng `1` na laman nito, modulo 2. Sa halimbawa sa ibaba, nagdaragdag ang sender ng parity bit sa bawat data block para maging even ang kabuuang bilang ng `1` sa resultang codeword.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Data (7 bits) | Number of 1s | Parity bit | Codeword with even parity (8 bits) |\n"
"|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n"
"| `0000000` | 0 | 0 | `00000000` |\n"
"| `1010001` | 3 | 1 | `10100011` |\n"
"| `1101001` | 4 | 0 | `11010010` |\n"
"| `1111111` | 7 | 1 | `11111111` |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Data (7 bits) | Bilang ng 1s | Parity bit | Codeword na may even parity (8 bits) |\n"
"|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n"
"| `0000000` | 0 | 0 | `00000000` |\n"
"| `1010001` | 3 | 1 | `10100011` |\n"
"| `1101001` | 4 | 0 | `11010010` |\n"
"| `1111111` | 7 | 1 | `11111111` |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The receiver recomputes the parity of the 8-bit codeword and rejects it if the parity is no longer even. A single bit-flip during transmission changes the parity by one and is flagged; two flips leave the parity unchanged and slip through undetected.\n"
msgstr "Muling kinukwenta ng receiver ang parity ng 8-bit na codeword at tinatanggihan ito kapag hindi na even ang parity. Ang isang bit-flip habang ipinapadala ay nagpapabago sa parity ng isa at nate-detect; ang dalawang flip naman ay nag-iiwan ng parity na hindi nagbabago kaya hindi ito napapansin.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The same idea, a structural rule on the bitstring that valid messages must satisfy, applies to the measurement outcomes of a quantum circuit. Parity is one such rule, but it is not the only one. More generally, when the ideal output distribution of a circuit is restricted by some structural constraint, that constraint can be used as the check: any measured bitstring that violates it can be rejected in classical post-processing as likely corrupted.\n"
msgstr "Ang parehong ideya, isang structural na patakaran sa bitstring na dapat sundin ng mga valid na mensahe, ay naaangkop sa mga resulta ng pagsukat ng isang quantum circuit. Ang parity ay isa lamang sa mga ganitong patakaran, hindi ito ang nag-iisa. Sa pangkalahatan, kapag ang ideal na output distribution ng isang circuit ay nililimitahan ng isang structural constraint, magagamit ang constraint na iyon bilang check: anumang nasukat na bitstring na lumalabag dito ay maaaring tanggihan sa classical post-processing bilang malamang na sira.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Such constraints typically originate from a [*symmetry*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetry_in_quantum_mechanics) of the ideal state; in other words, an operator $S$ that leaves the state invariant ($S|\\psi\\rangle = |\\psi\\rangle$), so that measurement outcomes must respect the constraint associated with $S$. For example, the GHZ state $(|0^n\\rangle + |1^n\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$ is stabilized by the pairwise parity operators $Z_i Z_j$, which enforce the all-agree computational-basis support $\\{0^n, 1^n\\}$; it is also stabilized by $X^{\\otimes n}$, which fixes the relative $+$ phase between the two components. As another example, in an occupation-number encoding of an electronic-structure problem, particle-number conservation restricts measurement outcomes to a fixed sector, so outcomes outside that sector can be rejected in classical post-processing as unphysical. This is why the technique is commonly called *symmetry post-selection*. It requires no extra qubits, no encoding, and no syndrome extraction, and thus the original circuit is run unchanged.\n"
msgstr "Karaniwang nagmumula ang mga ganitong constraint sa isang [*symmetry*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetry_in_quantum_mechanics) ng ideal na state; sa madaling salita, isang operator na $S$ na nag-iiwan sa state na hindi nagbabago ($S|\\psi\\rangle = |\\psi\\rangle$), kaya dapat sundin ng mga resulta ng pagsukat ang constraint na kaugnay ng $S$. Halimbawa, ang GHZ state na $(|0^n\\rangle + |1^n\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$ ay istabilisado ng mga pairwise parity operator na $Z_i Z_j$, na nagpapatupad ng all-agree na computational-basis support na $\\{0^n, 1^n\\}$; istabilisado rin ito ng $X^{\\otimes n}$, na nag-aayos sa relatibong $+$ phase sa pagitan ng dalawang component. Bilang isa pang halimbawa, sa occupation-number encoding ng isang electronic-structure problem, nililimitahan ng particle-number conservation ang mga resulta ng pagsukat sa isang nakatakdang sector, kaya ang mga resultang nasa labas ng sector na iyon ay maaaring tanggihan sa classical post-processing bilang hindi pisikal. Kaya karaniwang tinatawag ang teknik na ito na *symmetry post-selection*. Hindi ito nangangailangan ng dagdag na qubit, encoding, o syndrome extraction, kaya tumatakbo nang walang pagbabago ang orihinal na circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The simplicity comes with two costs. First, the check operates entirely on the post-measurement record: errors that are visible in the bitstring can be flagged, but the quantum information itself is consumed by the final measurement before any check is applied, so the technique cannot protect a state for further use. Second, it requires that the ideal output distribution have a known structure that can be tested directly on a bitstring; for example, the two-bitstring support of a GHZ state, or a fixed particle-number sector of a chemistry ansatz. Most deep computational circuits do not come with such diagnostic structure, in which case no symmetry rule is available. The technique is best viewed as a low-overhead first line of defense for circuits whose ideal output is well understood, rather than a general-purpose detection method.\n"
msgstr "Ang pagiging simple nito ay may dalawang kapalit. Una, ang check ay gumagana nang buo sa post-measurement record: ang mga error na nakikita sa bitstring ay maaaring i-flag, ngunit ang quantum information mismo ay nagagamit na ng huling pagsukat bago pa maisagawa ang anumang check, kaya hindi maprotektahan ng teknik ang isang state para magamit pa sa susunod. Pangalawa, kailangan nitong may kilalang istruktura ang ideal na output distribution na maaaring subukan nang direkta sa isang bitstring; halimbawa, ang two-bitstring support ng isang GHZ state, o isang nakatakdang particle-number sector ng isang chemistry ansatz. Karamihan sa mga malalalim na computational circuit ay walang ganitong diagnostic na istruktura, kaya walang magagamit na symmetry rule. Pinakamainam na ituring ang teknik na ito bilang mababang-overhead na unang linya ng depensa para sa mga circuit na ang ideal na output ay lubos na nauunawaan, hindi bilang pangkalahatang paraan ng detection.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Even so, symmetry post-selection is an instructive entry point: it is simple enough to expose the core trade-offs of post-selected detection (post-selection rate, sampling overhead, and fidelity) that recur throughout the rest of the notebook. We define these quantities next.\n"
msgstr "Gayunpaman, ang symmetry post-selection ay isang kapaki-pakinabang na panimulang punto: sapat itong simple para ipakita ang mga pangunahing trade-off ng post-selected detection (post-selection rate, sampling overhead, at fidelity) na paulit-ulit na lalabas sa natitirang bahagi ng notebook. Ilalarawan natin ang mga ito sa susunod.\n"

