# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[*Hellinger fidelity*](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/quantum_info#hellinger_fidelity) between two probability distributions $p$ and $q$ on $n$-bit strings:\n"
msgstr "[*Hellinger fidelity*](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/quantum_info#hellinger_fidelity) sa pagitan ng dalawang probability distribution na $p$ at $q$ sa mga $n$-bit string:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "    $$F(p, q) = \\bigg(\\sum_i \\sqrt{p_i\\, q_i}\\,\\bigg)^2,$$\n"
msgstr "    $$F(p, q) = \\bigg(\\sum_i \\sqrt{p_i\\, q_i}\\,\\bigg)^2,$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  where the sum runs over all $n$-bit strings $i$, and $p_i$ and $q_i$ are the probabilities that $p$ and $q$ assign to $i$. $F = 1$ for identical distributions and $F = 0$ when no bitstring is assigned positive probability by both distributions. $F$ is a classical distribution-level quantity computed *after* measurement and does not certify the full quantum state; for example, a GHZ state and its phase-flipped counterpart can produce identical computational-basis distributions and therefore identical $F$. We return to this potential obstacle when motivating stabilizer-code checks in [Section 1.2](#stabilizer-code).\n"
"- *Detection region* of a check: the set of circuit locations at which a fault can flip that check; which Pauli types are caught at each location depends on the check. The final checks of Part 1 can only respond to faults whose effects survive to the final measurements, and whether a surviving fault is actually detected depends on the particular check. Part 2 places checks whose regions are deliberately narrower and traces them through the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""
"  kung saan ang sum ay tumatakbo sa lahat ng $n$-bit string na $i$, at ang $p_i$ at $q_i$ ay ang mga probabilidad na itinatalaga ng $p$ at $q$ sa $i$. Ang $F = 1$ para sa magkaparehong distribution at ang $F = 0$ kapag walang bitstring na may positibong probabilidad sa parehong distribution. Ang $F$ ay isang klasikal na quantity sa antas ng distribution na kinukwenta *pagkatapos* ng pagsukat at hindi nito pinatutunayan ang buong quantum state; halimbawa, ang isang GHZ state at ang phase-flipped na katapat nito ay maaaring magbunga ng magkaparehong computational-basis distribution at samakatuwid ay magkaparehong $F$. Babalikan natin ang posibleng hadlang na ito kapag ipinapaliwanag ang motibasyon para sa mga stabilizer-code check sa [Seksyon 1.2](#stabilizer-code).\n"
"- *Detection region* ng isang check: ang hanay ng mga lokasyon sa circuit kung saan maaaring baligtarin ng isang fault ang check na iyon; kung aling mga uri ng Pauli ang nahuhuli sa bawat lokasyon ay depende sa check. Ang mga huling check ng Part 1 ay maaari lamang tumugon sa mga fault na ang epekto ay umaabot hanggang sa mga huling pagsukat, at kung ang isang fault na umabot ay talagang nade-detect ay depende sa partikular na check. Ang Part 2 ay naglalagay ng mga check na sadyang mas makitid ang mga region at sinusundan ang mga ito sa buong circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1.1 GHZ as a benchmark for symmetry filtering {#111-ghz-as-a-benchmark-for-symmetry-filtering}"
msgstr "1.1.1 GHZ bilang benchmark para sa symmetry filtering {#111-ghz-as-a-benchmark-for-symmetry-filtering}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We use the $n$-qubit GHZ state\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\mathrm{GHZ}_n\\rangle = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n} + |1\\rangle^{\\otimes n}\\big).\n"
"$$\n"
"In the computational basis, the ideal outcome distribution is supported on exactly two bitstrings, $0^n$ and $1^n$. Any other bitstring can be rejected in classical post-processing as inconsistent with this support.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ginagamit natin ang $n$-qubit na GHZ state\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\mathrm{GHZ}_n\\rangle = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n} + |1\\rangle^{\\otimes n}\\big).\n"
"$$\n"
"Sa computational basis, ang ideal na outcome distribution ay nakabatay sa eksaktong dalawang bitstring, ang $0^n$ at $1^n$. Anumang ibang bitstring ay maaaring tanggihan sa classical post-processing bilang hindi tugma sa support na ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For our run we use $n = 10$. Both ideal outputs $0^{10}$ and $1^{10}$ have an even number of `1`s (0 and 10, respectively), so we compare two rules on the same dataset:\n"
msgstr "Para sa ating run, ginagamit natin ang $n = 10$. Parehong may even na bilang ng `1` ang dalawang ideal na output na $0^{10}$ at $1^{10}$ (0 at 10, ayon sa pagkakasunod), kaya ikinukumpara natin ang dalawang patakaran sa parehong dataset:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Even-parity rule.* Keep shots whose number of `1`s is even. This rule applies only when $n$ is even (the case here); for odd $n$ the two ideal outputs have opposite parity, and no single parity rule keeps both.\n"
msgstr "*Even-parity rule.* Panatilihin ang mga shot na even ang bilang ng `1`. Naaangkop lamang ang patakarang ito kapag even ang $n$ (ang kaso rito); para sa odd na $n$, magkasalungat ang parity ng dalawang ideal na output, at walang iisang parity rule na nagpapanatili sa pareho.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*All-agree rule.* Keep shots that are exactly $0^n$ or $1^n$.\n"
msgstr "*All-agree rule.* Panatilihin ang mga shot na eksaktong $0^n$ o $1^n$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The all-agree rule is more restrictive than even parity: every all-agree bitstring has an even number of `1`s, but most bitstrings with an even number of `1`s are not all-agree. Even parity catches any odd number of bit flips but lets through any pair of compensating flips. Under noise, we therefore expect the all-agree rule to give higher post-selected fidelity at a lower post-selection rate.\n"
msgstr "Mas mahigpit ang all-agree rule kaysa sa even parity: bawat all-agree na bitstring ay may even na bilang ng `1`, ngunit karamihan sa mga bitstring na may even na bilang ng `1` ay hindi all-agree. Nahuhuli ng even parity ang anumang odd na bilang ng bit flip ngunit pinapalusot ang anumang pares ng magkakansela-sa-isa't-isa na flip. Sa ilalim ng ingay, inaasahan natin kung gayon na ang all-agree rule ay magbibigay ng mas mataas na post-selected fidelity sa mas mababang post-selection rate.\n"

