msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/2a7ded50-0.avif)\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Use the plot to compare the Hellinger fidelity and acceptance of the two filters. The all-agree rule keeps a subset of the even-parity shots, so its acceptance cannot be higher. This is the accuracy-throughput trade-off introduced by post-selection: stricter filters can make the accepted distribution cleaner, but they also reduce the number of usable samples and therefore raise the sampling overhead. Here both filters preserve the full ideal GHZ support, so Hellinger fidelity cannot decrease as the accepted set shrinks, provided some shots remain. Every shot the all-agree rule keeps lies in the ideal support by construction, so its Hellinger fidelity falls short of 1 only when $0^n$ and $1^n$ are not equally frequent. This distribution-level improvement does not certify phase coherence.\n"
msgstr "Gamitin ang plot para ikumpara ang Hellinger fidelity at acceptance ng dalawang filter. Pinananatili ng all-agree rule ang isang subset ng mga even-parity na shot, kaya hindi maaaring mas mataas ang acceptance nito. Ito ang accuracy-throughput trade-off na dala ng post-selection: ang mas mahihigpit na filter ay maaaring maglinis sa tinanggap na distribution, ngunit binabawasan din nila ang bilang ng magagamit na sample at kaya tumataas ang sampling overhead. Dito, pinapanatili ng parehong filter ang buong ideal na GHZ support, kaya hindi maaaring bumaba ang Hellinger fidelity habang lumiliit ang tinanggap na set, basta may natitirang mga shot. Ang bawat shot na pinananatili ng all-agree rule ay nasa ideal na support sa konstruksyon, kaya mas mababa lamang sa 1 ang Hellinger fidelity nito kapag hindi magkasingdalas ang $0^n$ at $1^n$. Ang pagbuting ito sa antas ng distribution ay hindi nagpapatunay ng phase coherence.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Even parity is good at catching any odd number of bit flips, but it has a structural flaw: any pair of compensating flips slips through. The next subsection addresses this issue.\n"
"<span id=\"parity\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Mahusay ang even parity sa pagtuklas ng anumang odd na bilang ng bit flip, ngunit may istruktural itong kapintasan: anumang pares ng magkakanselang flip ay nakakalusot. Tinutugunan ito ng susunod na subseksyon.\n"
"<span id=\"parity\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1.2 When parity is not enough {#112-when-parity-is-not-enough}"
msgstr "1.1.2 Kapag hindi sapat ang parity {#112-when-parity-is-not-enough}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Even parity catches any odd number of bit flips but lets through any even number of flips. We can quantify this directly from the hardware run above: every shot that satisfies the even-parity rule but is *not* in the all-agree set $\\{0^{10}, 1^{10}\\}$ is a corrupted shot that an even number of bit flips moved into the same parity sector as the ideal output.\n"
msgstr "Nahuhuli ng even parity ang anumang odd na bilang ng bit flip ngunit pinapalusot ang anumang even na bilang ng flip. Maaari natin itong sukatin nang direkta mula sa hardware run sa itaas: bawat shot na pumapasa sa even-parity rule ngunit *wala* sa all-agree set na $\\{0^{10}, 1^{10}\\}$ ay isang sirang shot na inilipat ng even na bilang ng bit flip sa parehong parity sector ng ideal na output.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The false-accept rate measures how often the even-parity rule accepts an output outside the ideal support.\n"
msgstr "Sinusukat ng false-accept rate kung gaano kadalas tumatanggap ang even-parity rule ng output na nasa labas ng ideal na support.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The previous analysis exposes a more fundamental limitation: a final computational-basis filter only sees what is recorded in the bit values. Errors that change bit values (bit-flip errors) leave a trace there and can in principle be filtered. Errors that change only the *phase* between basis states (phase-flip errors) can rotate the relative phase between, say, $|0^n\\rangle$ and $|1^n\\rangle$ without changing the bitstring distribution, so no rule applied only to the final bitstrings can detect them.\n"
msgstr "Inilalantad ng nakaraang pagsusuri ang isang mas pundamental na limitasyon: ang isang huling computational-basis filter ay nakikita lamang ang nakatala sa mga halaga ng bit. Ang mga error na nagbabago ng halaga ng bit (bit-flip error) ay nag-iiwan ng bakas doon at maaaring i-filter sa prinsipyo. Ang mga error na nagbabago lamang ng *phase* sa pagitan ng mga basis state (phase-flip error) ay maaaring paikutin ang relatibong phase sa pagitan ng, halimbawa, $|0^n\\rangle$ at $|1^n\\rangle$ nang hindi binabago ang bitstring distribution, kaya walang patakarang inilalapat lamang sa mga huling bitstring ang makaka-detect sa mga ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This limitation motivates the next section. To detect phase-flip errors, the check itself must access information that is not visible in the bitstring, which is what stabilizer codes provide.\n"
msgstr "Ang limitasyong ito ang nag-uudyok sa susunod na seksyon. Para ma-detect ang mga phase-flip error, dapat makakuha ang check mismo ng impormasyong hindi nakikita sa bitstring, na siyang ibinibigay ng mga stabilizer code.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "Subukan ang iyong pang-unawa {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose you prepare a three-qubit state whose ideal outcomes are only `000` and `111`. What rule would you use for post-selection, and what single-qubit errors would that rule catch?\n"
msgstr "Ipagpalagay na nagha-handa ka ng three-qubit na state na ang mga ideal na outcome ay `000` at `111` lamang. Anong patakaran ang gagamitin mo para sa post-selection, at anong mga single-qubit error ang mahuhuli ng patakarang iyon?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Sagot\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The natural choice is the *all-agree rule*: keep only `000` and `111`. The even-parity rule does not work here, because `000` has zero $1$s and `111` has three, so their parities are opposite. Any single bit-flip ($X$) error breaks the all-agree pattern, so the rule catches every weight-$1$ bit-flip. It does *not* catch phase-flip ($Z$) errors, which leave bit values unchanged. The phase-flip problem motivates [Section 1.2](#stabilizer-code).\n"
msgstr "Ang natural na pagpipilian ay ang *all-agree rule*: panatilihin lamang ang `000` at `111`. Hindi gumagana rito ang even-parity rule, dahil ang `000` ay may zero na $1$ at ang `111` ay may tatlo, kaya magkasalungat ang kanilang parity. Anumang single bit-flip ($X$) na error ay sumisira sa all-agree na pattern, kaya nahuhuli ng patakaran ang bawat weight-$1$ na bit-flip. *Hindi* nito nahuhuli ang mga phase-flip ($Z$) na error, na nag-iiwan ng mga halaga ng bit na hindi nagbabago. Ang problema sa phase-flip ang nag-uudyok sa [Seksyon 1.2](#stabilizer-code).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"<span id=\"stabilizer-code\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.2 Stabilizer-code post-selection: the $[[4,2,2]]$ code {#12-stabilizer-code-post-selection-the-422-code}"
msgstr "1.2 Stabilizer-code post-selection: ang $[[4,2,2]]$ code {#12-stabilizer-code-post-selection-the-422-code}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Section 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection) left two open limitations. First, the bitstring rules used there only see what is recorded in the [computational basis](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/multiple-systems/quantum-information#measurements-of-quantum-states), that is, the eigenbasis of the $Z$ operator. They cannot see phase-flip errors, which are invisible in that basis. (Changing the measurement basis can make different error information visible, but no single final measurement basis gives a complete, non-destructive check of both bit- and phase-type errors for an arbitrary encoded state.) Second, applying the check requires measuring the data qubits, which collapses the state and consumes the information we wanted to keep. The classical fix to the second problem, keeping redundant copies and checking that they agree, is unavailable here because the [no-cloning theorem](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/limitations-on-quantum-information#no-cloning-theorem) forbids duplicating an unknown quantum state.\n"
msgstr "Nag-iwan ang [Seksyon 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection) ng dalawang bukas na limitasyon. Una, ang mga bitstring rule na ginamit doon ay nakikita lamang ang nakatala sa [computational basis](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/multiple-systems/quantum-information#measurements-of-quantum-states), ibig sabihin, ang eigenbasis ng operator na $Z$. Hindi nila nakikita ang mga phase-flip error, na hindi nakikita sa basis na iyon. (Ang pagpapalit ng measurement basis ay maaaring maglantad ng ibang impormasyon tungkol sa error, ngunit walang iisang huling measurement basis na nagbibigay ng kumpleto at non-destructive na check ng parehong bit- at phase-type na error para sa isang arbitrary na encoded state.) Pangalawa, ang paglalapat ng check ay nangangailangan ng pagsukat sa mga data qubit, na nagpapa-collapse sa state at umuubos sa impormasyong gusto nating panatilihin. Ang klasikal na solusyon sa pangalawang problema, ang pagpapanatili ng mga redundant na kopya at pagsuri kung magkapareho ang mga ito, ay hindi magagamit dito dahil ipinagbabawal ng [no-cloning theorem](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/limitations-on-quantum-information#no-cloning-theorem) ang pagdoble ng isang hindi kilalang quantum state.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Why no-cloning matters here\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Bakit mahalaga ang no-cloning dito\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The [no-cloning theorem](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/limitations-on-quantum-information#no-cloning-theorem) says there is no unitary $U$ that takes an arbitrary unknown state $|\\psi\\rangle$ together with a fresh register and produces $|\\psi\\rangle \\otimes |\\psi\\rangle$. Cloning is a *non-linear* operation in $|\\psi\\rangle$, but every quantum operation is linear: if $U$ cloned the two basis states $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$, linearity would force $U$ to send the superposition $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ to $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle|1\\rangle)$, which is *not* the cloned state $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle) \\otimes \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$. The contradiction arises specifically because the input is a superposition; cloning basis states alone is fine.\n"
msgstr "Sinasabi ng [no-cloning theorem](/learning/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/limitations-on-quantum-information#no-cloning-theorem) na walang unitary na $U$ na kumukuha ng arbitrary na hindi kilalang state na $|\\psi\\rangle$ kasama ng isang bagong register at gumagawa ng $|\\psi\\rangle \\otimes |\\psi\\rangle$. Ang cloning ay isang *non-linear* na operasyon sa $|\\psi\\rangle$, ngunit linear ang bawat quantum operation: kung na-clone ng $U$ ang dalawang basis state na $|0\\rangle$ at $|1\\rangle$, pipilitin ng linearity ang $U$ na ipadala ang superposition na $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ sa $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle|1\\rangle)$, na *hindi* ang na-clone na state na $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle) \\otimes \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$. Lumilitaw ang kontradiksyon dahil mismo sa superposition ang input; ayos lang ang pag-clone ng mga basis state lamang.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This does not forbid preparing multiple copies of a state we *already know how to prepare*: we can simply run the preparation procedure on fresh registers. A CNOT cleanly copies computational-basis information, sending $|a\\rangle|0\\rangle \\mapsto |a\\rangle|a\\rangle$ for $a \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, but this does not extend to arbitrary superpositions. The encoding circuits used by stabilizer codes combine the logical input, which may be unknown, with initialized ancillas and distribute its information across an encoded register without making copies. What no-cloning rules out is duplicating a state that arrives unknown from an earlier computation.\n"
msgstr "Hindi nito ipinagbabawal ang paghahanda ng maraming kopya ng isang state na *alam na natin kung paano ihanda*: maaari lamang nating patakbuhin ang preparation procedure sa mga bagong register. Malinis na kinokopya ng isang CNOT ang computational-basis na impormasyon, na nagpapadala ng $|a\\rangle|0\\rangle \\mapsto |a\\rangle|a\\rangle$ para sa $a \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, ngunit hindi ito umaabot sa mga arbitrary na superposition. Pinagsasama ng mga encoding circuit na ginagamit ng mga stabilizer code ang logical input, na maaaring hindi kilala, at ang mga initialized na ancilla, at ipinapamahagi ang impormasyon nito sa isang encoded register nang hindi gumagawa ng mga kopya. Ang ipinagbabawal ng no-cloning ay ang pagdoble ng isang state na dumarating na hindi kilala mula sa naunang computation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Stabilizer codes resolve both limitations at once. The [logical state](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/stabilizer-formalism/repetition-code-revisited#syndromes) is encoded across multiple physical qubits through entanglement, and an ancilla qubit couples to the data to report a stabilizer eigenvalue, enough to flag whether the state has left the [codespace](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/stabilizer-formalism/stabilizer-codes), but not enough to reveal the logical amplitudes. By using two complementary stabilizers, one diagonal in the $Z$ basis and one in the $X$ basis, the check is sensitive to both bit-flip and phase-flip errors. Because only the ancilla is measured, this can in principle be applied without collapsing the encoded state. Used this way, the code is post-selected quantum error correction in its original form: an error-detecting code whose flagged shots are discarded rather than corrected. The reach of the check is bounded by the *code distance* $d$: with $d=2$, the $[[4,2,2]]$ code we use here detects every weight-$1$ Pauli error on a data qubit but is blind to certain weight-$2$ errors, including the even-bit-flip patterns that already escaped the parity rule in [Section 1.1.2](#parity).\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Sabay na nilulutas ng mga stabilizer code ang parehong limitasyon. Ang [logical state](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/stabilizer-formalism/repetition-code-revisited#syndromes) ay ine-encode sa maraming physical qubit sa pamamagitan ng entanglement, at ang isang ancilla qubit ay kumakabit sa data para iulat ang eigenvalue ng isang stabilizer, sapat para i-flag kung umalis na ang state sa [codespace](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/stabilizer-formalism/stabilizer-codes), ngunit hindi sapat para ibunyag ang mga logical amplitude. Sa paggamit ng dalawang komplementaryong stabilizer, isang diagonal sa $Z$ basis at isa sa $X$ basis, ang check ay sensitibo sa parehong bit-flip at phase-flip na error. Dahil ang ancilla lamang ang sinusukat, maaari itong ilapat sa prinsipyo nang hindi pinapa-collapse ang encoded na state. Sa ganitong paggamit, ang code ay post-selected quantum error correction sa orihinal nitong anyo: isang error-detecting code na ang mga na-flag na shot ay itinatapon sa halip na itinatama. Ang saklaw ng check ay nililimitahan ng *code distance* na $d$: sa $d=2$, ang $[[4,2,2]]$ code na ginagamit natin dito ay nagde-detect ng bawat weight-$1$ na Pauli error sa isang data qubit ngunit bulag sa ilang weight-$2$ na error, kabilang ang mga even-bit-flip na pattern na nakalusot na sa parity rule sa [Seksyon 1.1.2](#parity).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Quick refresher on stabilizer-code terminology\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Mabilis na pagbabalik-aral sa terminolohiya ng stabilizer-code\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The terms collected below are the ones used most often in this section. For full details, refer to the IBM Quantum Learning [*Stabilizer codes*](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/stabilizer-formalism/stabilizer-codes) course.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga terminong nakalap sa ibaba ang pinakamadalas gamitin sa seksyong ito. Para sa buong detalye, tingnan ang IBM Quantum Learning course na [*Stabilizer codes*](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/stabilizer-formalism/stabilizer-codes).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Physical qubit, logical qubit.* The hardware qubits used to store encoded information are the physical qubits. The abstract qubits whose state is being protected are the logical qubits.\n"
msgstr "*Physical qubit, logical qubit.* Ang mga hardware qubit na ginagamit para mag-imbak ng encoded na impormasyon ay ang mga physical qubit. Ang mga abstract na qubit na ang state ay pinoprotektahan ay ang mga logical qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Stabilizer generators.* A set of mutually commuting multi-qubit Pauli operators $P_1, \\ldots, P_r$. The states satisfying $P_k|\\psi\\rangle = |\\psi\\rangle$ for every $k$ form the codespace.\n"
msgstr "*Stabilizer generators.* Isang hanay ng mga multi-qubit na Pauli operator na $P_1, \\ldots, P_r$ na nagko-commute sa isa't isa. Ang mga state na tumutupad sa $P_k|\\psi\\rangle = |\\psi\\rangle$ para sa bawat $k$ ay bumubuo sa codespace.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Codespace.* The simultaneous $+1$ eigenspace of the stabilizer generators.\n"
msgstr "*Codespace.* Ang sabay-sabay na $+1$ eigenspace ng mga stabilizer generator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Ancilla qubit.* An auxiliary qubit coupled to the data qubits to extract a stabilizer eigenvalue without measuring the data directly.\n"
msgstr "*Ancilla qubit.* Isang auxiliary na qubit na kumakabit sa mga data qubit para kunin ang eigenvalue ng isang stabilizer nang hindi direktang sinusukat ang data.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Syndrome.* The string of stabilizer-measurement outcomes, under the convention that outcome `0` corresponds to eigenvalue $+1$ and outcome `1` to eigenvalue $-1$.\n"
msgstr "*Syndrome.* Ang string ng mga resulta ng stabilizer measurement, sa ilalim ng convention na ang outcome na `0` ay tumutugma sa eigenvalue na $+1$ at ang outcome na `1` sa eigenvalue na $-1$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*$[[n, k, d]]$ notation.* A stabilizer code on $n$ physical qubits that encodes $k$ logical qubits with code distance $d$.\n"
msgstr "*$[[n, k, d]]$ notation.* Isang stabilizer code sa $n$ physical qubit na nag-e-encode ng $k$ logical qubit na may code distance na $d$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Weight of a Pauli operator.* The number of qubits on which the operator acts as something other than the identity.\n"
msgstr "*Weight ng isang Pauli operator.* Ang bilang ng mga qubit na kung saan kumikilos ang operator bilang anumang bagay maliban sa identity.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Logical operator.* A Pauli operator that commutes with every stabilizer generator and is not proportional to a stabilizer element.\n"
msgstr "*Logical operator.* Isang Pauli operator na nagko-commute sa bawat stabilizer generator at hindi proporsyonal sa isang elemento ng stabilizer.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Code distance $d$.* The minimum weight of a logical operator.\n"
msgstr "*Code distance $d$.* Ang pinakamababang weight ng isang logical operator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"The $[[4,2,2]]$ code uses $n=4$ physical qubits to encode $k=2$ logical qubits with code distance $d=2$. Its codespace is the simultaneous $+1$ eigenspace of two stabilizer generators,\n"
"$$\n"
"Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0.\n"
"$$\n"
"A single-qubit $X$ error anti-commutes with $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ and is flagged by the corresponding syndrome bit; a single-qubit $Z$ error anti-commutes with $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$ and is flagged by the other. Every weight-$1$ Pauli error on a data qubit is detected by at least one of the two stabilizers. The two-bit syndrome identifies the error type ($X$, $Z$, $Y$, or none) but not the qubit on which the error occurred. With $d=2$, this information is insufficient to determine a correction, so the $[[4,2,2]]$ code is used as a detection-only code.\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Ang $[[4,2,2]]$ code ay gumagamit ng $n=4$ na physical qubit para i-encode ang $k=2$ na logical qubit na may code distance na $d=2$. Ang codespace nito ay ang sabay-sabay na $+1$ eigenspace ng dalawang stabilizer generator,\n"
"$$\n"
"Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0.\n"
"$$\n"
"Ang isang single-qubit na $X$ error ay anti-commute sa $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ at ina-flag ng kaukulang syndrome bit; ang isang single-qubit na $Z$ error ay anti-commute sa $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$ at ina-flag ng isa pa. Bawat weight-$1$ na Pauli error sa isang data qubit ay dine-detect ng hindi bababa sa isa sa dalawang stabilizer. Tinutukoy ng two-bit na syndrome ang uri ng error ($X$, $Z$, $Y$, o wala) ngunit hindi ang qubit kung saan nangyari ang error. Sa $d=2$, hindi sapat ang impormasyong ito para matukoy ang pagwawasto, kaya ang $[[4,2,2]]$ code ay ginagamit bilang detection-only na code.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The experiments below run syndrome extraction on the logical Bell state:\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|00\\rangle_L + |11\\rangle_L\\big),\n"
"$$\n"
"a state in the codespace.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga eksperimento sa ibaba ay nagsasagawa ng syndrome extraction sa logical Bell state:\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|00\\rangle_L + |11\\rangle_L\\big),\n"
"$$\n"
"isang state sa codespace.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Logical basis states of the [[4,2,2]] code\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Mga logical basis state ng [[4,2,2]] code\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The $[[4,2,2]]$ code encodes two logical qubits using four physical qubits. As with any two-qubit system, there are four logical computational-basis states, $|00\\rangle_L$, $|01\\rangle_L$, $|10\\rangle_L$, $|11\\rangle_L$, read as the values of the two encoded qubits (for example, $|00\\rangle_L$ has both logical qubits in the logical-zero state). What makes this an *encoding* is that each $|ab\\rangle_L$ is a specific superposition of physical four-qubit basis states, chosen so that all four logical basis states satisfy the codespace conditions $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0 = +1$ and $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0 = +1$.\n"
msgstr "Ang $[[4,2,2]]$ code ay nag-e-encode ng dalawang logical qubit gamit ang apat na physical qubit. Tulad ng anumang two-qubit na sistema, may apat na logical computational-basis state, $|00\\rangle_L$, $|01\\rangle_L$, $|10\\rangle_L$, $|11\\rangle_L$, na binabasa bilang mga halaga ng dalawang encoded na qubit (halimbawa, ang $|00\\rangle_L$ ay may parehong logical qubit sa logical-zero na state). Ang ginagawang *encoding* ito ay ang bawat $|ab\\rangle_L$ ay isang partikular na superposition ng mga physical four-qubit basis state, na pinili para tumupad ang lahat ng apat na logical basis state sa mga kondisyon ng codespace na $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0 = +1$ at $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0 = +1$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The standard choice is\n"
msgstr "Ang karaniwang pagpipilian ay\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"|00\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0000\\rangle + |1111\\rangle\\big), \\\\\n"
"|01\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0011\\rangle + |1100\\rangle\\big), \\\\\n"
"|10\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0101\\rangle + |1010\\rangle\\big), \\\\\n"
"|11\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0110\\rangle + |1001\\rangle\\big).\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"|00\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0000\\rangle + |1111\\rangle\\big), \\\\\n"
"|01\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0011\\rangle + |1100\\rangle\\big), \\\\\n"
"|10\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0101\\rangle + |1010\\rangle\\big), \\\\\n"
"|11\\rangle_L &= \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0110\\rangle + |1001\\rangle\\big).\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each line pairs two four-qubit strings related by flipping all four bits. Both strings in each line have an even number of $1$s. Since $Z$ has eigenvalue $+1$ on $|0\\rangle$ and $-1$ on $|1\\rangle$, the eigenvalue of $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ on a computational-basis string is $(-1)^{\\text{number of }1\\text{s}}$, so a string with an even number of $1$s gives eigenvalue $+1$. The $+$ combination is also invariant under $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$, which simply permutes the two terms within each line.\n"
msgstr "Bawat linya ay nagpapares ng dalawang four-qubit string na magkaugnay sa pamamagitan ng pag-flip ng lahat ng apat na bit. Parehong may even na bilang ng $1$ ang dalawang string sa bawat linya. Dahil ang $Z$ ay may eigenvalue na $+1$ sa $|0\\rangle$ at $-1$ sa $|1\\rangle$, ang eigenvalue ng $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ sa isang computational-basis string ay $(-1)^{\\text{number of }1\\text{s}}$, kaya ang string na may even na bilang ng $1$ ay nagbibigay ng eigenvalue na $+1$. Ang $+$ na kombinasyon ay invariant din sa ilalim ng $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$, na nagpapalit lamang ng dalawang term sa loob ng bawat linya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The logical Bell state used in the experiments is:\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|00\\rangle_L + |11\\rangle_L\\big),\n"
"$$\n"
"the analog of the standard Bell state $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle)$ on the two logical qubits. Substituting the codeword expressions above gives its physical four-qubit form:\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\big(|0000\\rangle + |1111\\rangle + |0110\\rangle + |1001\\rangle\\big).\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang logical Bell state na ginamit sa mga eksperimento ay:\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|00\\rangle_L + |11\\rangle_L\\big),\n"
"$$\n"
"ang katumbas ng karaniwang Bell state na $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle)$ sa dalawang logical qubit. Ang pagpapalit ng mga expression ng codeword sa itaas ay nagbibigay ng physical four-qubit na anyo nito:\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\big(|0000\\rangle + |1111\\rangle + |0110\\rangle + |1001\\rangle\\big).\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The preparation circuit applies two H + CNOT pairs, on $(q_0, q_3)$ and $(q_1, q_2)$. Each pair produces a physical Bell state $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle)$ on its two qubits, so the full four-qubit output is the tensor product:\n"
"$$\n"
"\\tfrac{1}{2}\\big(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle\\big)_{q_0 q_3} \\otimes \\big(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle\\big)_{q_1 q_2}.\n"
"$$\n"
"Expanding this tensor product, the four computational-basis strings that appear with equal amplitude are $|0000\\rangle, |1111\\rangle, |0110\\rangle, |1001\\rangle$, the same four strings that appear in the expression for $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ above. The circuit therefore prepares $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ directly.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang preparation circuit ay naglalapat ng dalawang H + CNOT na pares, sa $(q_0, q_3)$ at $(q_1, q_2)$. Bawat pares ay gumagawa ng physical Bell state na $\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle)$ sa dalawang qubit nito, kaya ang buong four-qubit na output ay ang tensor product:\n"
"$$\n"
"\\tfrac{1}{2}\\big(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle\\big)_{q_0 q_3} \\otimes \\big(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle\\big)_{q_1 q_2}.\n"
"$$\n"
"Sa pagpapalawak ng tensor product na ito, ang apat na computational-basis string na lumilitaw na may pantay na amplitude ay $|0000\\rangle, |1111\\rangle, |0110\\rangle, |1001\\rangle$, ang parehong apat na string na lumilitaw sa expression para sa $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ sa itaas. Kaya direktang inihahanda ng circuit ang $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We start by building the circuit that prepares the logical Bell state $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$. The construction uses two H + CNOT pairs to create two physical Bell pairs: one between data qubits 0 and 3, the other between data qubits 1 and 2. Their tensor product lies inside the $[[4,2,2]]$ codespace and is equal to $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ as defined in the previous section.\n"
msgstr "Sinisimulan natin sa pagbuo ng circuit na naghahanda sa logical Bell state na $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$. Gumagamit ang konstruksyon ng dalawang H + CNOT na pares para lumikha ng dalawang physical Bell pair: isa sa pagitan ng mga data qubit 0 at 3, at ang isa pa sa pagitan ng mga data qubit 1 at 2. Ang tensor product nila ay nasa loob ng $[[4,2,2]]$ codespace at katumbas ng $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ ayon sa depinisyon sa nakaraang seksyon.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/850496ab-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/850496ab-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we extract both syndromes simultaneously. The $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ check is implemented by applying CNOTs from each data qubit into a single ancilla, so the ancilla parity records the joint $Z$-eigenvalue. The $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$ check uses Hadamard conjugation: applying H gates on the data qubits before and after the CNOTs converts the $X$-parity check into a $Z$-parity check on a rotated frame, which the same CNOT pattern then extracts onto a second ancilla.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay kinukuha natin ang parehong syndrome nang sabay. Ang $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ check ay ipinapatupad sa pamamagitan ng paglalapat ng mga CNOT mula sa bawat data qubit papunta sa iisang ancilla, kaya itinatala ng parity ng ancilla ang pinagsamang $Z$-eigenvalue. Ang $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$ check ay gumagamit ng Hadamard conjugation: ang paglalapat ng mga H gate sa mga data qubit bago at pagkatapos ng mga CNOT ay nagko-convert sa $X$-parity check tungo sa $Z$-parity check sa isang rotated na frame, na saka kinukuha ng parehong CNOT pattern papunta sa pangalawang ancilla.\n"

