msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/0dda3dbd-0.avif)\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.2.1 Simulator validation {#121-simulator-validation}"
msgstr "1.2.1 Simulator validation {#121-simulator-validation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Before running on hardware, we verify the syndrome extraction on a noiseless simulator. We prepare the logical Bell state $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ and measure both stabilizers, $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ and $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$, onto separate ancillas. Since $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ lies in the codespace, both syndrome bits should return `0` deterministically.\n"
msgstr "Bago tumakbo sa hardware, bine-verify natin ang syndrome extraction sa isang noiseless na simulator. Hinahanda natin ang logical Bell state na $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ at sinusukat ang parehong stabilizer, $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ at $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$, papunta sa magkahiwalay na ancilla. Dahil ang $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ ay nasa codespace, parehong dapat magbalik ng `0` nang deterministiko ang dalawang syndrome bit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Both syndrome bits return `0` deterministically on the clean logical Bell state, confirming that $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ lies in the simultaneous $+1$ eigenspace of $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ and $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$, that is, in the codespace.\n"
msgstr "Parehong nagbabalik ng `0` nang deterministiko ang dalawang syndrome bit sa malinis na logical Bell state, na nagpapatunay na ang $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ ay nasa sabay-sabay na $+1$ eigenspace ng $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ at $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$, ibig sabihin, nasa codespace.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Before moving to hardware, we walk through how the two stabilizers respond to single-qubit Pauli errors. A stabilizer measurement returns $-1$ (syndrome bit `1`) when the error operator anti-commutes with the stabilizer, and $+1$ (syndrome bit `0`) when it commutes. This follows from how Pauli operators act on eigenstates: if $S|\\psi\\rangle = +|\\psi\\rangle$ and $E$ anti-commutes with $S$ ($ES = -SE$), then $S(E|\\psi\\rangle) = -E S|\\psi\\rangle = -E|\\psi\\rangle$, so the corrupted state $E|\\psi\\rangle$ is a $-1$ eigenstate of $S$.\n"
msgstr "Bago lumipat sa hardware, tinitingnan natin kung paano tumutugon ang dalawang stabilizer sa mga single-qubit na Pauli error. Ang isang stabilizer measurement ay nagbabalik ng $-1$ (syndrome bit na `1`) kapag anti-commute ang error operator sa stabilizer, at $+1$ (syndrome bit na `0`) kapag nagko-commute ito. Ito ay sumusunod sa kung paano kumikilos ang mga Pauli operator sa mga eigenstate: kung $S|\\psi\\rangle = +|\\psi\\rangle$ at ang $E$ ay anti-commute sa $S$ ($ES = -SE$), kung gayon $S(E|\\psi\\rangle) = -E S|\\psi\\rangle = -E|\\psi\\rangle$, kaya ang sirang state na $E|\\psi\\rangle$ ay isang $-1$ eigenstate ng $S$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We verify this directly on the simulator by injecting $X$, $Z$, and $Y$ errors on different data qubits and reading both syndrome bits.\n"
msgstr "Bine-verify natin ito nang direkta sa simulator sa pamamagitan ng pag-inject ng mga $X$, $Z$, at $Y$ na error sa iba't ibang data qubit at pagbasa sa parehong syndrome bit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The simulation confirms the anti-commutation analysis: every single-qubit $X$ error gives the same syndrome regardless of which data qubit it hits, and similarly for $Z$ and $Y$. The four possible outcomes of the two-bit syndrome correspond cleanly to four error classes (the table below lists the syndrome bits as $(s_X, s_Z)$, matching the displayed [bitstring order convention in Qiskit](/guides/bit-ordering) `c[1] c[0]`):\n"
msgstr "Kinukumpirma ng simulation ang anti-commutation analysis: ang bawat single-qubit na $X$ error ay nagbibigay ng parehong syndrome kahit aling data qubit ang tamaan, at gayundin para sa $Z$ at $Y$. Ang apat na posibleng resulta ng two-bit na syndrome ay malinaw na tumutugma sa apat na klase ng error (inililista ng talahanayan sa ibaba ang mga syndrome bit bilang $(s_X, s_Z)$, na tugma sa ipinapakitang [bitstring order convention sa Qiskit](/guides/bit-ordering) na `c[1] c[0]`):\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Error on any single data qubit | $s_X$ | $s_Z$ |\n"
"|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n"
"| no error | `0` | `0` |\n"
"| $X$ | `0` | `1` |\n"
"| $Z$ | `1` | `0` |\n"
"| $Y$ | `1` | `1` |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Error sa alinmang single data qubit | $s_X$ | $s_Z$ |\n"
"|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n"
"| walang error | `0` | `0` |\n"
"| $X$ | `0` | `1` |\n"
"| $Z$ | `1` | `0` |\n"
"| $Y$ | `1` | `1` |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two limitations of this scheme are worth stating now. First, the table identifies the *type* of error ($X$, $Z$, $Y$, or none) but not the *qubit* on which the error occurred: an $X$ error on data qubit 0 and an $X$ error on data qubit 2 produce identical syndromes, as the simulation shows directly. Without knowing the qubit location we cannot apply a corresponding inverse to undo the error, which is why we use the $[[4,2,2]]$ code for detection only: we post-select on $s_X = s_Z = 0$ and do not attempt correction. Second, the table covers only single-qubit errors; certain weight-2 errors commute with both stabilizers and escape the syndrome entirely. We return to that limitation in [Section 1.3](#limitations).\n"
msgstr "Dalawang limitasyon ng scheme na ito ang dapat banggitin ngayon. Una, tinutukoy ng talahanayan ang *uri* ng error ($X$, $Z$, $Y$, o wala) ngunit hindi ang *qubit* kung saan nangyari ang error: ang isang $X$ error sa data qubit 0 at ang isang $X$ error sa data qubit 2 ay gumagawa ng magkaparehong syndrome, gaya ng direktang ipinapakita ng simulation. Kung hindi natin alam ang lokasyon ng qubit, hindi tayo makakapaglapat ng katumbas na inverse para bawiin ang error, kaya ginagamit natin ang $[[4,2,2]]$ code para sa detection lamang: nagpo-post-select tayo sa $s_X = s_Z = 0$ at hindi sinusubukang magwasto. Pangalawa, sumasaklaw lamang ang talahanayan sa mga single-qubit na error; may ilang weight-2 na error na nagko-commute sa parehong stabilizer at ganap na nakakalusot sa syndrome. Babalikan natin ang limitasyong iyon sa [Seksyon 1.3](#limitations).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-1}"
msgstr "Subukan ang iyong pang-unawa {#check-your-understanding-1}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We just saw that an $X$ error flips the $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ syndrome, and a $Z$ error would flip the $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ syndrome. What happens if a $Y$ error hits a single data qubit? Which stabilizer or stabilizers would detect it?\n"
msgstr "Nakita lang natin na ang isang $X$ error ay nagpa-flip sa $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ syndrome, at ang isang $Z$ error ay magpa-flip sa $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ syndrome. Ano ang mangyayari kung ang isang $Y$ error ay tumama sa isang data qubit? Aling stabilizer o mga stabilizer ang makaka-detect nito?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Hint:* $Y = iXZ$, so a $Y$ error is both a bit-flip and a phase-flip on the same qubit.\n"
msgstr "*Hint:* $Y = iXZ$, kaya ang isang $Y$ error ay parehong bit-flip at phase-flip sa iisang qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Since $Y = iXZ$, it anti-commutes with both $Z$-type and $X$-type stabilizer operators on the affected qubit. A single $Y$ error therefore triggers both stabilizers at once, giving syndrome $s_Z = 1$ and $s_X = 1$. The four single-qubit Pauli outcomes (none, $X$, $Z$, $Y$) map to four distinct syndrome patterns, as the simulation above confirms.\n"
msgstr "Dahil $Y = iXZ$, ito ay anti-commute sa parehong $Z$-type at $X$-type na stabilizer operator sa apektadong qubit. Kaya ang isang $Y$ error ay nagti-trigger sa parehong stabilizer nang sabay, na nagbibigay ng syndrome na $s_Z = 1$ at $s_X = 1$. Ang apat na single-qubit na Pauli outcome (wala, $X$, $Z$, $Y$) ay tumutugma sa apat na magkakaibang syndrome pattern, gaya ng kinukumpirma ng simulation sa itaas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.2.2 Hardware demonstration: joint $Z$ and $X$ syndrome extraction {#122-hardware-demonstration-joint-z-and-x-syndrome-extraction}"
msgstr "1.2.2 Hardware demonstration: pinagsamang $Z$ at $X$ syndrome extraction {#122-hardware-demonstration-joint-z-and-x-syndrome-extraction}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We now run the same circuit on hardware and quantify the trade-off introduced by post-selecting on $s_X = s_Z = 0$. The metrics we report are the post-selection rate $A$ (fraction of shots that pass both checks), the sampling overhead $1/A$, and the Hellinger fidelity to the ideal data-qubit distribution before and after post-selection.\n"
msgstr "Pinapatakbo na natin ngayon ang parehong circuit sa hardware at sinusukat ang trade-off na dala ng pagpo-post-select sa $s_X = s_Z = 0$. Ang mga metric na iniuulat natin ay ang post-selection rate na $A$ (ang bahagi ng mga shot na pumasa sa parehong check), ang sampling overhead na $1/A$, at ang Hellinger fidelity sa ideal na data-qubit distribution bago at pagkatapos ng post-selection.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The hardware circuit reuses the syndrome-extraction circuit `qc_check` built in the simulator validation above and adds measurements on the four data qubits at the end, in keeping with the end-of-circuit detection scope in Part 1. The two registers are kept separate: the syndrome bits land in `c` ($s_Z = c[0]$, $s_X = c[1]$), and the four data bits land in a new register `data_c`.\n"
msgstr "Ginagamit muli ng hardware circuit ang syndrome-extraction circuit na `qc_check` na binuo sa simulator validation sa itaas at nagdaragdag ng mga pagsukat sa apat na data qubit sa dulo, alinsunod sa end-of-circuit detection scope sa Part 1. Magkahiwalay ang dalawang register: ang mga syndrome bit ay napupunta sa `c` ($s_Z = c[0]$, $s_X = c[1]$), at ang apat na data bit ay napupunta sa bagong register na `data_c`.\n"

