msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/061ffe29-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Submit the circuit. Wait for completion before proceeding.\n"
msgstr "Isumite ang circuit. Hintayin ang pagkumpleto bago magpatuloy.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parse the six-bit results into the two syndromes ($s_Z$, $s_X$) and the four data bits, then apply each post-selection filter independently.\n"
msgstr "I-parse ang anim na-bit na resulta sa dalawang syndrome ($s_Z$, $s_X$) at apat na data bit, pagkatapos ay ilapat nang hiwalay ang bawat post-selection filter.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/a446654c-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/a446654c-1.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the saved run, post-selection improves the measured Hellinger fidelity, with the highest value obtained when both syndromes pass. Requiring both checks also gives the lowest acceptance, so the improvement comes at the cost of fewer usable shots. This measures agreement of the final Z-basis distribution with the ideal distribution, rather than certifying the full quantum state. On another backend or calibration, compare the fidelity and acceptance again. Requiring both syndromes always keeps a subset of the shots accepted by either check alone, but the fidelity ordering can change.\n"
msgstr "Sa naka-save na run, pinapabuti ng post-selection ang nasukat na Hellinger fidelity, at ang pinakamataas na halaga ay nakukuha kapag pumasa ang parehong syndrome. Ang pag-require sa parehong check ay nagbibigay din ng pinakamababang acceptance, kaya ang pagbuti ay may kapalit na mas kaunting magagamit na shot. Sinusukat nito ang pagkakatugma ng huling Z-basis distribution sa ideal na distribution, at hindi nito pinatutunayan ang buong quantum state. Sa ibang backend o calibration, ikumpara muli ang fidelity at acceptance. Ang pag-require sa parehong syndrome ay palaging nagpapanatili ng subset ng mga shot na tinatanggap ng alinmang check nang mag-isa, ngunit maaaring magbago ang pagkakasunod-sunod ng fidelity.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-2}"
msgstr "Subukan ang iyong pang-unawa {#check-your-understanding-2}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose $X$ errors strike both qubit $0$ and qubit $1$ simultaneously ($X_0 X_1$). What syndrome would $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ produce? Would $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ catch it? Would the error be detected at all?\n"
msgstr "Ipagpalagay na ang mga $X$ error ay sabay na tumama sa qubit $0$ at qubit $1$ ($X_0 X_1$). Anong syndrome ang gagawin ng $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$? Mahuhuli ba ito ng $X_3X_2X_1X_0$? Made-detect ba talaga ang error?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$: each $X$ flips one qubit's contribution to the $Z$-eigenvalue, and two flips cancel, so $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ returns $+1$ (syndrome bit `0`). For $X_3X_2X_1X_0$: $X_0 X_1$ commutes with $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ (both are products of $X$s), so $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ also returns $+1$. The error goes undetected. This is the $d=2$ shortcoming: $X_0 X_1$ is a weight-$2$ logical operator that maps one valid codeword to another without leaving the codespace.\n"
msgstr "Para sa $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$: bawat $X$ ay nagpa-flip sa kontribusyon ng isang qubit sa $Z$-eigenvalue, at ang dalawang flip ay nagkakansela, kaya ang $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ ay nagbabalik ng $+1$ (syndrome bit na `0`). Para sa $X_3X_2X_1X_0$: ang $X_0 X_1$ ay nagko-commute sa $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ (parehong produkto ng mga $X$), kaya ang $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ ay nagbabalik din ng $+1$. Hindi na-detect ang error. Ito ang kakulangan ng $d=2$: ang $X_0 X_1$ ay isang weight-$2$ na logical operator na naglilipat ng isang valid na codeword sa isa pa nang hindi umaalis sa codespace.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-3}"
msgstr "Subukan ang iyong pang-unawa {#check-your-understanding-3}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Consider two errors: $X_0$ (single bit-flip on qubit $0$) and $X_1$ (single bit-flip on qubit $1$). Both are weight-$1$ errors that the $[[4,2,2]]$ code does detect. Can the code also tell them apart, that is, can it identify *which* qubit was hit?\n"
msgstr "Isaalang-alang ang dalawang error: $X_0$ (single bit-flip sa qubit $0$) at $X_1$ (single bit-flip sa qubit $1$). Parehong weight-$1$ na error ang mga ito na dine-detect ng $[[4,2,2]]$ code. Maaari rin bang paghiwalayin ng code ang dalawa, ibig sabihin, matutukoy ba nito *kung aling* qubit ang tinamaan?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "No. Both $X_0$ and $X_1$ produce the same syndrome, $s_Z = 1$ and $s_X = 0$. The two-bit syndrome identifies that an $X$-type error happened, but not *which* qubit was affected. Equivalently, $X_0$ and $X_1$ differ by the logical operator $\\bar X_1 = X_0 X_1$, and logical operators are invisible to stabilizer measurements, so $X_0$ and $X_1 = X_0 \\cdot (X_0 X_1)$ look identical to the syndrome.\n"
msgstr "Hindi. Parehong gumagawa ang $X_0$ at $X_1$ ng parehong syndrome, $s_Z = 1$ at $s_X = 0$. Tinutukoy ng two-bit na syndrome na may nangyaring $X$-type na error, ngunit hindi *kung aling* qubit ang naapektuhan. Katumbas nito, ang $X_0$ at $X_1$ ay magkaiba sa pamamagitan ng logical operator na $\\bar X_1 = X_0 X_1$, at ang mga logical operator ay hindi nakikita ng mga stabilizer measurement, kaya ang $X_0$ at $X_1 = X_0 \\cdot (X_0 X_1)$ ay magkamukha sa syndrome.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is why we use the $[[4,2,2]]$ code for detection only: it cannot correct an arbitrary single-qubit error. Codes with $d \\geq 3$, such as the Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ code, can correct any single-qubit error with ideal syndrome extraction.\n"
msgstr "Kaya ginagamit natin ang $[[4,2,2]]$ code para sa detection lamang: hindi nito maitatama ang isang arbitrary na single-qubit na error. Ang mga code na may $d \\geq 3$, tulad ng Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ code, ay maaaring magtama ng anumang single-qubit na error kapag ideal ang syndrome extraction.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"The post-selection filter keeps shots where both syndrome bits read $0$, but passing the filter is not the same as correctness: some accepted shots can still land outside the ideal support of $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$. The cell below extracts these *false-accept* shots from the hardware data and breaks them into two categories: those whose data bitstrings have even parity but lie outside the target support, and those with odd parity. These are categories of computational-basis outcomes, not a test of whether the quantum state was in the codespace.\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Pinapanatili ng post-selection filter ang mga shot kung saan parehong 0 ang mga syndrome bit, ngunit ang pagpasa sa filter ay hindi katumbas ng kawastuhan: ang ilang tinanggap na shot ay maaari pa ring mapunta sa labas ng ideal na support ng $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$. Ang cell sa ibaba ay kumukuha ng mga *false-accept* na shot na ito mula sa hardware data at hinahati ang mga ito sa dalawang kategorya: ang mga data bitstring na may even parity ngunit nasa labas ng target na support, at ang mga may odd parity. Ito ay mga kategorya ng mga computational-basis outcome, hindi isang pagsubok kung ang quantum state ay nasa codespace.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The false-accept shots could have arisen from several mechanisms: readout errors on the data or ancilla qubits, gate errors in the syndrome circuit, weight-$2$ errors that the syndrome cannot detect, or non-Pauli errors such as energy relaxation, which a stabilizer measurement catches only with some probability. The \"closest ideal(s)\" column compares bitstrings, but it does not identify which of these mechanisms occurred or certify that the quantum state was in the codespace. [Section 1.3](#limitations) takes up these limits.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga false-accept na shot ay maaaring nagmula sa ilang mekanismo: mga readout error sa mga data o ancilla qubit, mga gate error sa syndrome circuit, mga weight-$2$ na error na hindi ma-detect ng syndrome, o mga non-Pauli na error tulad ng energy relaxation, na nahuhuli lamang ng stabilizer measurement sa isang tiyak na probabilidad. Ikinukumpara ng column na \"closest ideal(s)\" ang mga bitstring, ngunit hindi nito tinutukoy kung alin sa mga mekanismong ito ang naganap o pinatutunayan na ang quantum state ay nasa codespace. Tinatalakay ng [Seksyon 1.3](#limitations) ang mga limitasyong ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Readout error mitigation techniques such as M3 or TREX could be applied to reduce the readout contribution and obtain higher-fidelity post-selected results.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga teknik sa readout error mitigation tulad ng M3 o TREX ay maaaring ilapat para bawasan ang kontribusyon ng readout at makakuha ng mga post-selected na resultang may mas mataas na fidelity.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-4}"
msgstr "Subukan ang iyong pang-unawa {#check-your-understanding-4}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A stabilizer code has parameters $[[n, k, d]]$ with $r = n - k$ stabilizer generators. Suppose we want to encode more logical qubits at the same distance. Must the number of stabilizer generators increase, and what determines the syndrome-extraction cost?\n"
msgstr "Ang isang stabilizer code ay may mga parameter na $[[n, k, d]]$ na may $r = n - k$ na stabilizer generator. Ipagpalagay na gusto nating mag-encode ng mas maraming logical qubit sa parehong distance. Kailangan bang dumami ang bilang ng mga stabilizer generator, at ano ang tumutukoy sa gastos ng syndrome extraction?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not necessarily: $r = n - k$ need not increase with $k$. For example, even-$n$ codes with $n \\geq 4$ and generators $X^{\\otimes n}$ and $Z^{\\otimes n}$ have parameters $[[n,n-2,2]]$: they keep two generators while their weights grow. Syndrome-extraction cost depends on the number and weights of the generators, routing, and whether ancillas are reused. Two-qubit gates are typically a major noise source on near-term hardware, so a larger extraction circuit can introduce more noise and reduce the post-selection rate.\n"
msgstr "Hindi kinakailangan: ang $r = n - k$ ay hindi laging tumataas kasabay ng $k$. Halimbawa, ang mga code na may even na $n$ kung saan $n \\geq 4$ at may generators na $X^{\\otimes n}$ at $Z^{\\otimes n}$ ay may parameters na $[[n,n-2,2]]$: dalawa lang ang generators nila pero lumalaki ang weights. Ang gastos ng syndrome-extraction ay nakadepende sa dami at weights ng mga generator, sa routing, at kung ginagamit muli ang mga ancilla. Ang mga two-qubit gate ay karaniwang pangunahing pinagmumulan ng ingay sa near-term hardware, kaya ang mas malaking extraction circuit ay maaaring magdagdag ng ingay at magpababa ng post-selection rate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following examples illustrate different encoding rates and generator counts:\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita ng mga sumusunod na halimbawa ang iba't ibang encoding rate at bilang ng generator:\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Code | $n$ | $k$ | $d$ | Encoding rate $k/n$ | Stabilizer generators |\n"
"|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n"
"| $[[4,2,2]]$ | 4 | 2 | 2 | 0.50 | 2 |\n"
"| $[[5,1,3]]$ | 5 | 1 | 3 | 0.20 | 4 |\n"
"| $[[7,1,3]]$ (Steane) | 7 | 1 | 3 | 0.14 | 6 |\n"
"| $[[9,1,3]]$ (Shor) | 9 | 1 | 3 | 0.11 | 8 |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Code | $n$ | $k$ | $d$ | Encoding rate $k/n$ | Stabilizer generators |\n"
"|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n"
"| $[[4,2,2]]$ | 4 | 2 | 2 | 0.50 | 2 |\n"
"| $[[5,1,3]]$ | 5 | 1 | 3 | 0.20 | 4 |\n"
"| $[[7,1,3]]$ (Steane) | 7 | 1 | 3 | 0.14 | 6 |\n"
"| $[[9,1,3]]$ (Shor) | 9 | 1 | 3 | 0.11 | 8 |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In these examples, the distance-$3$ codes use more stabilizer generators and have lower encoding rates than the $[[4,2,2]]$ code. This comparison is not a universal scaling rule. The cost of code-based detection depends on the chosen code and its implementation.\n"
msgstr "Sa mga halimbawang ito, mas maraming stabilizer generator ang ginagamit ng mga distance-$3$ code at mas mababa ang encoding rate nila kaysa sa $[[4,2,2]]$ code. Hindi ito pangkalahatang scaling rule. Ang gastos ng code-based detection ay nakadepende sa napiling code at sa implementasyon nito.\n"

