msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.3 Limits of end-of-circuit detection {#13-limits-of-end-of-circuit-detection}"
msgstr "1.3 Mga limitasyon ng end-of-circuit detection {#13-limits-of-end-of-circuit-detection}"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "So far we have looked at end-of-circuit post-selection through two examples: bitstring filtering ([Section 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection)) and stabilizer-code post-selection ([Section 1.2](#stabilizer-code)). Three limitations follow from the architecture they share.\n"
msgstr "Sa ngayon, tiningnan natin ang end-of-circuit post-selection sa pamamagitan ng dalawang halimbawa: bitstring filtering ([Seksyon 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection)) at stabilizer-code post-selection ([Seksyon 1.2](#stabilizer-code)). May tatlong limitasyon na nagmumula sa arkitekturang pareho nilang ginagamit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Some errors are not flagged**\n"
msgstr "**May mga error na hindi na-flag**\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "With $d = 2$, weight-$2$ Pauli operators that act non-trivially on the encoded data can commute with both stabilizers and therefore go undetected. The reach is set by the code distance: detecting more errors of higher weight requires a code with larger $d$.\n"
msgstr "Kapag $d = 2$, ang mga weight-$2$ Pauli operator na kumikilos nang non-trivial sa encoded data ay maaaring mag-commute sa parehong stabilizer kaya hindi sila natutukoy. Ang saklaw ay itinatakda ng code distance: para makatukoy ng mas maraming error na mas mataas ang weight, kailangan ng code na mas malaki ang $d$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**The syndrome does not localize the fault**\n"
msgstr "**Hindi nililokalisa ng syndrome ang fault**\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When a syndrome bit fires, the syndrome record tells us that something went wrong somewhere in the circuit but not on which qubit, in which gate layer, or whether the underlying fault was a gate error in the payload, a gate error in the syndrome extraction, or a measurement readout error on the data or ancilla qubits. The single-shot ambiguity reported in the false-accept table (multiple ideal codewords often equally close in bitstring distance) is consistent with the same limit.\n"
msgstr "Kapag may syndrome bit na nag-fire, sinasabi ng syndrome record na may nagkamali sa kung saan sa circuit, pero hindi kung sa anong qubit, sa anong gate layer, o kung ang pinagbabatayang fault ay gate error sa payload, gate error sa syndrome extraction, o measurement readout error sa data o ancilla qubit. Ang single-shot na kalabuan na iniulat sa false-accept table (madalas na maraming ideal na codeword ang pantay-pantay ang lapit sa bitstring distance) ay tugma sa parehong limitasyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Detection itself adds gates**\n"
msgstr "**Nagdaragdag ng mga gate ang mismong detection**\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "At the logical level, the $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ preparation uses four gates (two H + two CNOT), and the joint $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ + $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$ syndrome extraction adds 16 logical gates (four CNOT for Z-check + 12 gates for X-check with Hadamard conjugation) and two ancilla qubits. However, transpilation to hardware-native gates increases these counts, as the cell below demonstrates.\n"
msgstr "Sa logical level, gumagamit ang $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ preparation ng apat na gate (dalawang H + dalawang CNOT), at ang joint $Z_3 Z_2 Z_1 Z_0$ + $X_3 X_2 X_1 X_0$ syndrome extraction ay nagdaragdag ng 16 na logical gate (apat na CNOT para sa Z-check + 12 gate para sa X-check na may Hadamard conjugation) at dalawang ancilla qubit. Gayunpaman, dinadagdagan pa ng transpilation papunta sa hardware-native na mga gate ang mga bilang na ito, gaya ng ipinapakita ng cell sa ibaba.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This example is intentionally extreme. In the $[[4,2,2]]$ code, the $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ preparation here uses only four logical gates, so the syndrome-extraction block makes up most of the transpiled circuit. The gate-count comparison above illustrates a large relative overhead. When the payload is minimal, syndrome extraction can dominate the total gate count. In a payload with many more gates, the same fixed-size syndrome block would be a smaller fraction of the total. The conclusion that the syndrome-extraction circuit is itself noisy still holds, even though the ratio above does not.\n"
msgstr "Sinadyang sukdulan ang halimbawang ito. Sa $[[4,2,2]]$ code, apat na logical gate lang ang ginagamit ng $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$ preparation dito, kaya ang syndrome-extraction block ang bumubuo sa karamihan ng transpiled circuit. Ipinapakita ng gate-count comparison sa itaas ang malaking relative overhead. Kapag minimal ang payload, maaaring mangibabaw ang syndrome extraction sa kabuuang bilang ng gate. Sa payload na mas maraming gate, mas maliit na bahagi ng kabuuan ang parehong fixed-size na syndrome block. Totoo pa rin na maingay mismo ang syndrome-extraction circuit, kahit hindi totoo ang ratio sa itaas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The cost depends on the code's stabilizer count and weights, routing, and ancilla reuse (note the scaling question above). As target circuits grow toward utility scale (40+ qubits), end-of-circuit code-based detection in this direct form might not be the most efficient option on near-term hardware.\n"
msgstr "Nakadepende ang gastos sa bilang at weights ng stabilizer ng code, sa routing, at sa muling paggamit ng ancilla (tingnan ang scaling question sa itaas). Habang lumalaki ang mga target circuit papunta sa utility scale (40+ qubit), maaaring hindi ang end-of-circuit code-based detection sa direktang anyong ito ang pinakamabisang opsyon sa near-term hardware.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 2: Mid-circuit detection {#part-2-mid-circuit-detection}"
msgstr "Part 2: Mid-circuit detection {#part-2-mid-circuit-detection}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Section 1.3](#limitations) listed three limitations of end-of-circuit detection: certain weight-2 errors go undetected, the syndrome does not localize the fault, and a fixed syndrome-extraction block is applied to every shot. In Part 2, we move detection inside the circuit and partially address the third limitation. Checks couple ancillas to the data at selected circuit locations, including interior points. In these examples, the ancillas are measured at the end alongside the data, and post-selection happens in classical post-processing. When lighter checks are distributed across multiple interior points, the per-shot gate cost can track the circuit structure rather than a fixed code block. Some faults still pass undetected, and these checks do not in general identify a unique fault location.\n"
msgstr "Naglista ang [Section 1.3](#limitations) ng tatlong limitasyon ng end-of-circuit detection: may ilang weight-2 na error na hindi natutukoy, hindi nililokalisa ng syndrome ang fault, at isang fixed na syndrome-extraction block ang inilalapat sa bawat shot. Sa Part 2, inililipat natin ang detection sa loob ng circuit at bahagyang tinutugunan ang ikatlong limitasyon. Ikinakabit ng mga check ang mga ancilla sa data sa mga piling lokasyon ng circuit, kasama ang mga interior point. Sa mga halimbawang ito, sinusukat ang mga ancilla sa dulo kasama ng data, at nangyayari ang post-selection sa classical post-processing. Kapag ang mas magagaan na check ay ipinamahagi sa maraming interior point, ang gastos ng gate kada shot ay maaaring sumunod sa estruktura ng circuit sa halip na sa fixed na code block. May ilang fault na hindi pa rin natutukoy, at sa pangkalahatan ay hindi tinutukoy ng mga check na ito ang natatanging lokasyon ng fault.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We work through two ideas:\n"
msgstr "Dalawang ideya ang pag-aaralan natin:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Coherent Pauli checks (Sections [2.2](#coherent)-[2.3](#spacetime)): the algebraic foundation. For any Clifford payload $U$ and post-payload check $R$, the pre-payload check $L = U^\\dagger R\\, U$ defines a valid check pair.\n"
msgstr "Coherent Pauli checks (Sections [2.2](#coherent)-[2.3](#spacetime)): ang algebraic na pundasyon. Para sa anumang Clifford payload na $U$ at post-payload check na $R$, ang pre-payload check na $L = U^\\dagger R\\, U$ ay bumubuo ng wastong check pair.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spacetime codes (Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment)): a hardware-aware refinement. Place lightweight checks at points in the circuit where the local stabilizer structure makes $L$ unnecessary, and trace their detection regions through the circuit graph.\n"
msgstr "Spacetime codes (Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment)): isang hardware-aware na pagpapahusay. Maglagay ng magagaan na check sa mga punto ng circuit kung saan ginagawang hindi na kailangan ng lokal na stabilizer structure ang $L$, at sundan ang kanilang mga detection region sa circuit graph.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Both CPC and spacetime codes are designed for *Clifford circuits* (or circuits dominated by Clifford gates). The space of valid checks shrinks exponentially as non-Clifford content increases ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, 2025, Supp. V](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)). The random Clifford payload used in Part 2 is entirely Clifford, which is why it is a convenient testbed.\n"
msgstr "Parehong dinisenyo ang CPC at spacetime codes para sa *Clifford circuits* (o mga circuit na pinangingibabawan ng mga Clifford gate). Exponential na lumiliit ang espasyo ng mga wastong check habang dumarami ang non-Clifford na nilalaman ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, 2025, Supp. V](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)). Ang random Clifford payload na ginamit sa Part 2 ay ganap na Clifford, kaya maginhawa itong testbed.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.1 From end-of-circuit filtering to mid-circuit checks {#21-from-end-of-circuit-filtering-to-mid-circuit-checks}"
msgstr "2.1 Mula sa end-of-circuit filtering patungo sa mid-circuit checks {#21-from-end-of-circuit-filtering-to-mid-circuit-checks}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "End-of-circuit post-selection collects all check information from a final measurement. The checks can only see the cumulative effect of errors across the entire circuit. Mid-circuit checks change *what* the detection can see. A check ancilla couples to the data at selected points during the circuit. In these examples it is measured at the end alongside the data, and post-selection rejects flagged shots in classical post-processing.\n"
msgstr "Kinokolekta ng end-of-circuit post-selection ang lahat ng impormasyon ng check mula sa huling measurement. Ang epekto lang na naipon ng mga error sa buong circuit ang nakikita ng mga check. Binabago ng mga mid-circuit check *kung ano* ang nakikita ng detection. Ang isang check ancilla ay kumakabit sa data sa mga piling punto habang tumatakbo ang circuit. Sa mga halimbawang ito, sinusukat ito sa dulo kasama ng data, at itinatapon ng post-selection ang mga na-flag na shot sa classical post-processing.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The framework for mid-circuit checks is *coherent Pauli checks (CPC)*, introduced in [Section 2.2](#coherent). CPC tells us *what* to check and *why* the check is guaranteed to pass on an error-free circuit. The challenge is making these checks practical. A CPC-derived check can involve a high-weight Pauli operator spanning many qubits, which on a connectivity-constrained device requires routing gates that add more noise than the check removes. [Martiel & Javadi-Abhari (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725) address this directly. *Spacetime codes* keep the same CPC algebra but constrain checks to be spatially local, spreading their sensitivity across many circuit layers instead of many qubits. This transition begins in Section 2.3.3 and is developed in Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment).\n"
msgstr "Ang framework para sa mga mid-circuit check ay ang *coherent Pauli checks (CPC)*, na ipinakilala sa [Section 2.2](#coherent). Sinasabi ng CPC kung *ano* ang susuriin at *bakit* garantisadong papasa ang check sa circuit na walang error. Ang hamon ay gawing praktikal ang mga check na ito. Ang check na hango sa CPC ay maaaring may kasamang high-weight na Pauli operator na sumasaklaw sa maraming qubit, na sa device na may limitadong connectivity ay nangangailangan ng mga routing gate na nagdaragdag ng mas maraming ingay kaysa sa naaalis ng check. Direktang tinutugunan ito ng [Martiel & Javadi-Abhari (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725). Pinananatili ng *Spacetime codes* ang parehong CPC algebra pero nililimitahan ang mga check na maging spatially local, at ipinapakalat ang sensitibidad nila sa maraming circuit layer sa halip na sa maraming qubit. Nagsisimula ang paglipat na ito sa Section 2.3.3 at nililinang sa Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"What &quot;mid-circuit&quot; really means in this lesson\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Ano talaga ang ibig sabihin ng &quot;mid-circuit&quot; sa araling ito\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The benefit of a mid-circuit check is not the timing of the measurement, but the fact that *the check couples to the circuit at an interior point*, which means that its detection region can be tailored to selected parts of the payload. When we say \"mid-circuit check,\" think \"interior-coupled check with a spacetime detection region.\"\n"
msgstr "Ang benepisyo ng mid-circuit check ay hindi ang oras ng measurement, kundi ang katotohanang *kumakabit ang check sa circuit sa isang interior point*, na nangangahulugang maaaring iangkop ang detection region nito sa mga piling bahagi ng payload. Kapag sinabi nating \"mid-circuit check,\" isipin ang \"interior-coupled check na may spacetime detection region.\"\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment) hardware experiment, each check ancilla couples to the payload at a few points along a target wire and is measured at the *end of the circuit* alongside the data qubits, not at an intermediate point with reset and reuse. Its sensitivity nevertheless extends *into the circuit interior* because the check operator propagates through the payload under conjugation (discussed in [Section 2.4](#paulice)). What we demonstrate is the spacetime-code principle, checks whose detection regions extend deep into the circuit's interior, even though the measurement happens at the boundary. A fully mid-circuit implementation (measure, reset, reuse) would further reduce idle time on ancillas and is an important engineering step. See [Lanes et al. (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658) for how dynamic circuit capabilities enable these protocols at scale.\n"
msgstr "Sa hardware experiment ng [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment), kumakabit ang bawat check ancilla sa payload sa ilang punto sa isang target wire at sinusukat ito sa *dulo ng circuit* kasama ng mga data qubit, hindi sa isang intermediate point na may reset at reuse. Gayunman, umaabot ang sensitibidad nito *hanggang sa loob ng circuit* dahil ang check operator ay napo-propagate sa payload sa ilalim ng conjugation (tinalakay sa [Section 2.4](#paulice)). Ang ipinapakita natin ay ang prinsipyo ng spacetime-code: mga check na ang detection region ay umaabot nang malalim sa loob ng circuit, kahit nangyayari ang measurement sa hangganan. Ang ganap na mid-circuit na implementasyon (measure, reset, reuse) ay lalo pang magbabawas ng idle time sa mga ancilla at isang mahalagang hakbang sa engineering. Tingnan ang [Lanes et al. (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658) kung paano ginagawang posible ng mga kakayahan ng dynamic circuit ang mga protocol na ito sa malaking sukat.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-5}"
msgstr "Suriin ang pag-unawa mo {#check-your-understanding-5}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A 40-qubit circuit has post-selection rate $A = 0.6$ with one mid-circuit check. How many raw shots do you need to collect 3,000 accepted shots? What happens to this number if you add a second independent check with the same per-check post-selection rate?\n"
msgstr "Ang isang 40-qubit na circuit ay may post-selection rate na $A = 0.6$ na may isang mid-circuit check. Ilang raw shot ang kailangan mong kolektahin para makakuha ng 3,000 tinanggap na shot? Ano ang mangyayari sa bilang na ito kung magdadagdag ka ng pangalawang independent na check na may parehong post-selection rate kada check?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "With one check at $A = 0.6$, the sampling overhead is $1/A \\approx 1.67$, so you need $3\\,000 / 0.6 = 5\\,000$ raw shots.\n"
msgstr "Sa isang check na $A = 0.6$, ang sampling overhead ay $1/A \\approx 1.67$, kaya kailangan mo ng $3\\,000 / 0.6 = 5\\,000$ raw shot.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "If a second independent check also passes 60% of shots, the combined post-selection rate is $A_\\text{combined} = 0.6 \\times 0.6 = 0.36$. Now you need $3\\,000 / 0.36 \\approx 8\\,334$ raw shots, about $1.67\\times$ the single-check case.\n"
msgstr "Kung ang pangalawang independent na check ay pumapasa rin sa 60% ng mga shot, ang pinagsamang post-selection rate ay $A_\\text{combined} = 0.6 \\times 0.6 = 0.36$. Ngayon kailangan mo ng $3\\,000 / 0.36 \\approx 8\\,334$ raw shot, mga $1.67\\times$ ng kaso na may iisang check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This illustrates that each additional check multiplies the sampling cost, which shows that adding checks is not free. The fidelity gain from each extra check needs to justify the additional loss in post-selection rate. [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment) examines this trade-off in real hardware data.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita nito na pinararami ng bawat karagdagang check ang gastos sa sampling, kaya hindi libre ang pagdaragdag ng mga check. Kailangang bigyang-katwiran ng fidelity gain mula sa bawat dagdag na check ang karagdagang pagkawala sa post-selection rate. Sinusuri ng [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment) ang trade-off na ito sa totoong hardware data.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(Note: this calculation assumes check outcomes are independent. In practice, checks may share sensitivity to the same noise sources, so the combined post-selection rate can differ from the simple product.)\n"
msgstr "(Tandaan: ipinapalagay ng kalkulasyong ito na independent ang mga resulta ng check. Sa praktika, maaaring pareho ang sensitibidad ng mga check sa iisang pinagmumulan ng ingay, kaya maaaring iba ang pinagsamang post-selection rate sa simpleng product.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"<span id=\"coherent\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.2 Coherent Pauli checks (CPC) {#22-coherent-pauli-checks-cpc}"
msgstr "2.2 Coherent Pauli checks (CPC) {#22-coherent-pauli-checks-cpc}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Coherent Pauli checks (CPC) provide a framework for what to check and why the check works.\n"
msgstr "Nagbibigay ang Coherent Pauli checks (CPC) ng framework para sa kung ano ang susuriin at kung bakit gumagana ang check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here we have a Clifford circuit $U$ (the *payload*) that we want to protect. We pick a Pauli operator $R$ and apply it to the payload's output through a controlled-Pauli gate from an ancilla. The pre-payload partner $L$, obtained by pulling $R$ back through $U$, is applied to the input through a second controlled-Pauli. In the ideal (error-free) circuit the two controlled-Paulis cancel coherently on the ancilla, so the ancilla measurement is deterministic. Errors between the two controlled-Paulis that anti-commute with the effective check operator break this cancellation and can flip the ancilla outcome.\n"
msgstr "Dito, mayroon tayong Clifford circuit na $U$ (ang *payload*) na gusto nating protektahan. Pumipili tayo ng Pauli operator na $R$ at inilalapat ito sa output ng payload sa pamamagitan ng controlled-Pauli gate mula sa isang ancilla. Ang pre-payload partner na $L$, na nakukuha sa pag-pull back ng $R$ sa $U$, ay inilalapat sa input sa pamamagitan ng pangalawang controlled-Pauli. Sa ideal (walang error) na circuit, nagkakansela nang coherent ang dalawang controlled-Pauli sa ancilla, kaya deterministic ang ancilla measurement. Ang mga error sa pagitan ng dalawang controlled-Pauli na nag-a-anti-commute sa epektibong check operator ay sumisira sa pagkansela at maaaring mag-flip ng resulta ng ancilla.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Two ways the deterministic outcome can arise\">\n"
msgstr "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Dalawang paraan kung paano nagkakaroon ng deterministic na resulta\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*State-preparation setting.* When $U$ is run on the standard input $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ and $R$ is chosen to be a stabilizer of the target output state $U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, then $R$ alone returns $+1$ on the ideal output. This is the case for the right-only checks in Section 2.3.3. An input stabilizer $Z_j$ pushed forward through the payload stabilizes the output state, so it returns $+1$ with no $L$ needed.\n"
msgstr "*State-preparation setting.* Kapag pinatakbo ang $U$ sa standard input na $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ at ang $R$ ay pinili bilang stabilizer ng target output state na $U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, ang $R$ lang ay nagbabalik ng $+1$ sa ideal na output. Ito ang kaso para sa mga right-only check sa Section 2.3.3. Ang input stabilizer na $Z_j$ na itinulak pasulong sa payload ay nagse-stabilize sa output state, kaya nagbabalik ito ng $+1$ nang hindi kailangan ang $L$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*General CPC setting.* $R$ need *not* be a stabilizer by itself. The guarantee comes from the pair $(L, R)$: the coherent $L$-$U$-$R$ sandwich returns the ancilla to its starting value on an error-free circuit. If $R$ is not a stabilizer of the output, measuring it alone need not give a deterministic result. The *consistency* between $L$ and $R$ is checked, not the eigenvalue of either operator individually.\n"
msgstr "*General CPC setting.* Ang $R$ ay *hindi* kailangang maging stabilizer nang mag-isa. Ang garantiya ay nagmumula sa pares na $(L, R)$: ibinabalik ng coherent na $L$-$U$-$R$ sandwich ang ancilla sa panimulang halaga nito sa circuit na walang error. Kung ang $R$ ay hindi stabilizer ng output, ang pagsukat dito nang mag-isa ay hindi kinakailangang magbigay ng deterministic na resulta. Ang sinusuri ay ang *consistency* ng $L$ at $R$, hindi ang eigenvalue ng alinman sa kanila nang hiwalay.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In both settings the detection mechanism is the same.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa parehong setting, pareho ang mekanismo ng detection.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The detection mechanism is anti-commutation: an error anti-commuting with the check flips its outcome from $+1$ to $-1$, while an error commuting with the check passes undetected. (Concretely, $R = Z_0 Z_1$ catches $X$ or $Y$ on qubits 0-1 because $XZ = -ZX$, but misses $Z$-type errors there because $Z$ commutes with itself.)\n"
msgstr "Ang mekanismo ng detection ay anti-commutation: ang error na nag-a-anti-commute sa check ay nagfi-flip ng resulta nito mula $+1$ patungong $-1$, habang ang error na nag-co-commute sa check ay hindi natutukoy. (Halimbawa, nahuhuli ng $R = Z_0 Z_1$ ang $X$ o $Y$ sa qubit 0-1 dahil $XZ = -ZX$, pero hindi nito nahuhuli ang mga $Z$-type na error doon dahil nag-co-commute ang $Z$ sa sarili nito.)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To see what this looks like in practice, and why it creates a scalability challenge, we build an example step by step.\n"
msgstr "Para makita kung ano ang hitsura nito sa praktika, at kung bakit lumilikha ito ng hamon sa scalability, bubuo tayo ng halimbawa hakbang-hakbang.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.2.1 CPC in practice: the cost of conjugation {#221-cpc-in-practice-the-cost-of-conjugation}"
msgstr "2.2.1 CPC sa praktika: ang gastos ng conjugation {#221-cpc-in-practice-the-cost-of-conjugation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We build a 20-qubit random Clifford payload using a hardware-friendly Clifford gate set (H, CZ, SX, S). The circuit has a *brickwork structure*: alternating layers of nearest-neighbor CZ entangling gates, interleaved with random single-qubit Clifford gates (randomly chosen sequences of SX and S). The depth parameter counts CZ layers and we use five layers for the examples in Sections [2.2](#coherent)-[2.3](#spacetime). Its nearest-neighbor structure can be mapped to a physical line. [Section 2.4](#paulice) extends this construction to a deeper payload for the Paulice benchmark.\n"
msgstr "Bubuo tayo ng 20-qubit na random Clifford payload gamit ang hardware-friendly na Clifford gate set (H, CZ, SX, S). May *brickwork structure* ang circuit: salitan na mga layer ng nearest-neighbor CZ entangling gate, na may pagitan ng mga random na single-qubit Clifford gate (mga random na napiling sequence ng SX at S). Binibilang ng depth parameter ang mga CZ layer at gumagamit tayo ng limang layer para sa mga halimbawa sa Sections [2.2](#coherent)-[2.3](#spacetime). Ang nearest-neighbor na estruktura nito ay maaaring i-map sa isang pisikal na linya. Pinalalawig ng [Section 2.4](#paulice) ang konstruksyong ito sa mas malalim na payload para sa Paulice benchmark.\n"

