# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.3.1 Anti-commutation rule {#231-anti-commutation-rule}"
msgstr "2.3.1 Anti-commutation rule {#231-anti-commutation-rule}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A CPC check detects an error if and only if the error *anti-commutes* with the check operator. For an error injected between $U_A$ and $U_B$, there are two equivalent ways to see it:\n"
msgstr "Natutukoy ng CPC check ang isang error kung at kung *nag-a-anti-commute* lang ang error sa check operator. Para sa error na ini-inject sa pagitan ng $U_A$ at $U_B$, may dalawang katumbas na paraan para makita ito:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*From $R$'s side:* the error propagates forward through $U_B$, arriving at $R$ as $U_B E U_B^\\dagger$. Detection depends on whether this commutes with $R$.\n"
msgstr "*Mula sa panig ng $R$:* nagpo-propagate pasulong ang error sa $U_B$, at dumarating sa $R$ bilang $U_B E U_B^\\dagger$. Nakadepende ang detection kung nag-co-commute ito sa $R$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*From $L$'s side:* the error propagates backward through $U_A^\\dagger$, arriving at $L$ as $U_A^\\dagger E U_A$. Detection depends on whether this commutes with $L$.\n"
msgstr "*Mula sa panig ng $L$:* nagpo-propagate paurong ang error sa $U_A^\\dagger$, at dumarating sa $L$ bilang $U_A^\\dagger E U_A$. Nakadepende ang detection kung nag-co-commute ito sa $L$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Since $L = U^\\dagger R U$, these two conditions are algebraically equivalent: $L$ and $R$ always agree. We verify this:\n"
msgstr "Dahil $L = U^\\dagger R U$, algebraically na magkatumbas ang dalawang kondisyong ito: laging magkasundo ang $L$ at $R$. Bine-verify natin ito:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$L$ and $R$ always agree on each error: what one detects, the other detects too. Whether a given error is caught is not simply a matter of distance from the check's support: the rule is anti-commutation between the check operator and the error at the location it is evaluated. Some errors near the support are caught, others commute with the check there and pass. Errors far from the support often propagate through too few gates to reach $R$ as a non-trivial operator, and so they pass as well.\n"
msgstr "Laging magkasundo ang $L$ at $R$ sa bawat error: kung ano ang natutukoy ng isa, natutukoy din ng isa pa. Hindi simpleng usapin ng distansya mula sa support ng check kung nahuhuli ang isang error: ang patakaran ay anti-commutation sa pagitan ng check operator at ng error sa lokasyong sinusuri. Ang ilang error malapit sa support ay nahuhuli, ang iba ay nag-co-commute sa check doon at pumapasa. Ang mga error na malayo sa support ay madalas na dumadaan sa napakakaunting gate para makarating sa $R$ bilang non-trivial na operator, kaya pumapasa rin sila.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we quantify this: each of the 20 qubits can suffer an $X$, $Y$, or $Z$ error, giving $20 \\times 3 = 60$ possible single-qubit errors at the mid-circuit location. How many does this single CPC pair catch?\n"
msgstr "Ngayon susukatin natin ito: ang bawat isa sa 20 qubit ay maaaring magkaroon ng $X$, $Y$, o $Z$ na error, kaya may $20 \\times 3 = 60$ posibleng single-qubit na error sa mid-circuit na lokasyon. Ilan ang nahuhuli ng iisang CPC pair na ito?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The detected fraction depends on the check's support at the error location. If the propagated check acts on $w$ of the $n$ data qubits there, it anti-commutes with two of $\\{X,Y,Z\\}$ on each supported qubit and commutes with all three on the others. This sweep therefore detects $2w$ of the $3n$ single-qubit Pauli faults, a fraction $2w/(3n)$. Read the support and detected count from the output above; they change with the payload and the chosen insertion point. The support at this slice is part of the check's detection region, the spacetime wires reached by its back-cumulant ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. III-A](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"
msgstr "Nakadepende ang nade-detect na bahagi sa support ng check sa lokasyon ng error. Kung ang napropagate na check ay kumikilos sa $w$ sa $n$ data qubit doon, nag-a-anti-commute ito sa dalawa sa $\\{X,Y,Z\\}$ sa bawat supported na qubit at nag-co-commute sa lahat ng tatlo sa iba. Kaya ang sweep na ito ay nakaka-detect ng $2w$ sa $3n$ single-qubit Pauli fault, isang bahaging $2w/(3n)$. Basahin ang support at ang bilang ng nade-detect mula sa output sa itaas; nagbabago ang mga ito depende sa payload at sa napiling insertion point. Ang support sa slice na ito ay bahagi ng detection region ng check, ang mga spacetime wire na naaabot ng back-cumulant nito ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. III-A](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Checks with larger support at this slice catch a larger fraction of this sweep. Additional check pairs at different positions can extend coverage, but each brings its own $L$ operator. The detection rule as stated covers Pauli errors; a general error is a combination of Paulis, and a coherent or non-unitary error is caught only with the probability carried by the components that anti-commute with the check.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga check na mas malaki ang support sa slice na ito ay nakakahuli ng mas malaking bahagi ng sweep na ito. Ang karagdagang check pair sa iba't ibang posisyon ay maaaring magpalawak ng coverage, pero may sarili itong operator na $L$ ang bawat isa. Sumasaklaw ang detection rule gaya ng nakasaad sa mga Pauli error; ang pangkalahatang error ay kombinasyon ng mga Pauli, at ang coherent o non-unitary na error ay nahuhuli lang ayon sa probabilidad na dala ng mga component na nag-a-anti-commute sa check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.3.2 One-sided CPC: reading $R$ from the data {#232-one-sided-cpc-reading-r-from-the-data}"
msgstr "2.3.2 One-sided CPC: pagbasa ng $R$ mula sa data {#232-one-sided-cpc-reading-r-from-the-data}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The compiled resource counts in Section 2.2.1 show why a valid CPC can still be expensive: one ancilla must reach the supports of both $L$ and $R$. Their weights count the controlled-Pauli couplings, while hardware connectivity and routing determine the additional cost. A lower-weight check need not give a shallower compiled circuit.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita ng mga compiled resource count sa Section 2.2.1 kung bakit maaari pa ring magastos ang isang wastong CPC: kailangang maabot ng isang ancilla ang mga support ng parehong $L$ at $R$. Binibilang ng mga weight nila ang mga controlled-Pauli coupling, habang ang hardware connectivity at routing ang tumutukoy sa karagdagang gastos. Ang check na mas mababa ang weight ay hindi kinakailangang magbigay ng mas mababaw na compiled circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In Section 2.5 of their [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), van den Berg et al. reduce that cost by moving the check qubit along a line and combining each controlled-Pauli gate with the following swap. Their construction shows why designing checks together with qubit motion can be cheaper than relying on a generic router. Their one-sided construction also removes controlled-$R$ gates by evaluating a Z-type $R$ from the final data bits; we illustrate that option below.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Implementation detail: phase compensation\">\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa Section 2.5 ng kanilang [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), binabawasan nina van den Berg et al. ang gastos na iyon sa pamamagitan ng paggalaw ng check qubit sa isang linya at pagsasama ng bawat controlled-Pauli gate sa sumusunod na swap. Ipinapakita ng konstruksyon nila kung bakit maaaring mas mura ang pagdidisenyo ng mga check kasabay ng galaw ng qubit kaysa umasa sa generic na router. Inaalis din ng one-sided na konstruksyon nila ang mga controlled-$R$ gate sa pamamagitan ng pagsusuri ng Z-type na $R$ mula sa mga final data bit; ipinapakita namin ang opsyong iyon sa ibaba.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Detalye ng implementasyon: phase compensation\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`Pauli.phase` from Qiskit stores an overall factor $(-i)^k$. The `apply_controlled_paulis` helper implements the tensor factors but omits that phase. Under control it becomes a relative phase on the ancilla, so we restore it with $S^\\dagger$, $Z$, or $S$ for exponents 1, 2, or 3. For a CPC pair, use `L.phase + R.phase` modulo four. The one-sided example below uses `L.phase` alone. See [Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supplementary Information §II-C and Fig. S3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725).\n"
msgstr "Nag-iimbak ang `Pauli.phase` mula sa Qiskit ng kabuuang factor na $(-i)^k$. Ipinapatupad ng helper na `apply_controlled_paulis` ang mga tensor factor pero hindi isinasama ang phase na iyon. Sa ilalim ng control, nagiging relative phase ito sa ancilla, kaya ibinabalik natin ito gamit ang $S^\\dagger$, $Z$, o $S$ para sa exponent na 1, 2, o 3. Para sa CPC pair, gamitin ang `L.phase + R.phase` modulo four. Ang one-sided na halimbawa sa ibaba ay gumagamit ng `L.phase` lang. Tingnan ang [Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supplementary Information §II-C at Fig. S3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"**One-sided checks**\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A CPC pair can also compare an ancilla result with the final data bits. As in [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), implement $L = U^\\dagger R U$ with controlled gates and evaluate $R$ classically when it is diagonal in the measurement basis. For the $R=Z_8Z_9$ example from [Section 2.2](#coherent), the syndrome is the input-check bit XOR the two output bits. Ideal shots give zero, while an $X$ fault on qubit 8 after the payload flips that syndrome.\n"
msgstr "Maaari ring ihambing ng CPC pair ang resulta ng ancilla sa mga final data bit. Gaya sa [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), ipatupad ang $L = U^\\dagger R U$ gamit ang mga controlled gate at suriin ang $R$ nang classical kapag diagonal ito sa measurement basis. Para sa halimbawang $R=Z_8Z_9$ mula sa [Section 2.2](#coherent), ang syndrome ay ang input-check bit XOR ang dalawang output bit. Ang mga ideal na shot ay nagbibigay ng zero, habang ang $X$ fault sa qubit 8 pagkatapos ng payload ay nagfi-flip ng syndrome na iyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The cell below reuses `apply_controlled_paulis` on the same payload, with no inverse and no controlled-$R$ gates. This preserves the ideal final Z-basis statistics, although measuring $L$ can change the underlying data state. It illustrates the check identity, rather than benchmarking its hardware benefit.\n"
msgstr "Ginagamit muli ng cell sa ibaba ang `apply_controlled_paulis` sa parehong payload, nang walang inverse at walang controlled-$R$ gate. Pinapanatili nito ang ideal na final Z-basis statistics, bagaman ang pagsukat sa $L$ ay maaaring magbago sa pinagbabatayang data state. Inilalarawan nito ang check identity, sa halip na i-benchmark ang benepisyo nito sa hardware.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measuring $L$ on an ancilla is a projective measurement of the payload. Unless $L$ is a stabilizer of the state at that point, the measurement collapses the state onto a random $\\pm 1$ eigenspace of $L$. The eigenvalue is then faithfully carried to $R$ (which is why the XOR still passes), but the payload that continues through the circuit is no longer the state the computation prepared. In this one-sided construction, the data state can change while the ideal statistics in the chosen measurement basis remain unchanged.\n"
msgstr "Ang pagsukat sa $L$ sa isang ancilla ay isang projective measurement ng payload. Maliban kung ang $L$ ay stabilizer ng state sa puntong iyon, ang measurement ay nagko-collapse sa state sa random na $\\pm 1$ eigenspace ng $L$. Tapat na dinadala ang eigenvalue sa $R$ (kaya pumapasa pa rin ang XOR), pero ang payload na nagpapatuloy sa circuit ay hindi na ang state na inihanda ng computation. Sa one-sided na konstruksyong ito, maaaring magbago ang data state habang nananatiling pareho ang ideal na statistics sa napiling measurement basis.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Section 2.3.3 takes a different simplification of the CPC identity, as follows: choose $L$ to be a known input stabilizer, so its physical check can be omitted. This leads to an interior stabilizer check.\n"
msgstr "Ang Section 2.3.3 ay gumagamit ng ibang pagpapasimple ng CPC identity, gaya ng sumusunod: piliin ang $L$ bilang kilalang input stabilizer, kaya maaaring alisin ang pisikal na check nito. Humahantong ito sa interior stabilizer check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.3.3 From CPC to spacetime codes: placing checks where they work {#233-from-cpc-to-spacetime-codes-placing-checks-where-they-work}"
msgstr "2.3.3 Mula CPC patungo sa spacetime codes: paglalagay ng mga check kung saan sila gumagana {#233-from-cpc-to-spacetime-codes-placing-checks-where-they-work}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two-sided CPC compares $L$ at the input with $R$ at the output. If $L$ is a known input stabilizer, its value supplies the reference and its physical check can be omitted, leaving a right-only check. The input $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ is stabilized by any product of $Z$ operators, such as $Z_j$ or the all-$Z$ operator. Choose $L=Z_j$ and propagate it forward through the payload:\n"
msgstr "Inihahambing ng two-sided CPC ang $L$ sa input sa $R$ sa output. Kung ang $L$ ay kilalang input stabilizer, ang halaga nito ang nagsisilbing reference at maaaring alisin ang pisikal na check nito, na nag-iiwan ng right-only check. Ang input na $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ ay sine-stabilize ng anumang product ng mga $Z$ operator, gaya ng $Z_j$ o ng all-$Z$ operator. Piliin ang $L=Z_j$ at i-propagate ito pasulong sa payload:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$R_j=U Z_j U^\\dagger,\\qquad R_j\\, U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}=U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}.$$\n"
msgstr "$$R_j=U Z_j U^\\dagger,\\qquad R_j\\, U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}=U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measuring $R_j$ at the output therefore gives $+1$ ideally. An arbitrary CPC choice of $R$ need not have a known value, so dropping its partner can produce random outcomes. Clifford propagation keeps each $R_j$ a single Pauli, and the circuit sets its type and support. Finding $R_j$ means computing classically what the payload does to $Z_j$: efficient at any depth for our Clifford payload, but in a general circuit with non-Clifford gates typically cheap only through the first layers. A right-only check therefore gives a general computation a solid start, not a way around classical simulation.\n"
msgstr "Kaya ang pagsukat sa $R_j$ sa output ay ideal na nagbibigay ng $+1$. Ang arbitraryong pagpili ng CPC na $R$ ay maaaring walang kilalang halaga, kaya ang pag-alis ng partner nito ay maaaring magbunga ng random na resulta. Pinananatili ng Clifford propagation na iisang Pauli ang bawat $R_j$, at ang circuit ang nagtatakda ng uri at support nito. Ang paghahanap sa $R_j$ ay nangangahulugang pagkompyut nang classical ng ginagawa ng payload sa $Z_j$: episyente sa anumang depth para sa Clifford payload natin, pero sa pangkalahatang circuit na may non-Clifford gate ay karaniwang mura lang hanggang sa mga unang layer. Kaya ang right-only check ay nagbibigay sa pangkalahatang computation ng matibay na simula, hindi paraan para iwasan ang classical simulation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-6}"
msgstr "Suriin ang pag-unawa mo {#check-your-understanding-6}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why not measure an arbitrary CPC pair $L$ and $R$ separately, then XOR their outcomes?\n"
msgstr "Bakit hindi na lang sukatin nang hiwalay ang arbitraryong CPC pair na $L$ at $R$, saka i-XOR ang mga resulta nila?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The XOR agrees ideally, but measuring $L$ can project the data onto a random eigenstate and disrupt later coherent computation. The coherent CPC sandwich avoids revealing either individual value. Measuring a known stabilizer is harmless ideally, while reconstructing a partner from compatible final data measurements, as above, preserves those measurement statistics.\n"
msgstr "Ideal na nagkakasundo ang XOR, pero ang pagsukat sa $L$ ay maaaring mag-project ng data sa random na eigenstate at makagambala sa susunod na coherent na computation. Iniiwasan ng coherent na CPC sandwich na ibunyag ang alinman sa mga indibidwal na halaga. Ang pagsukat sa kilalang stabilizer ay ideal na hindi nakapipinsala, habang ang pagbuo muli ng partner mula sa magkatugmang final data measurement, gaya ng nasa itaas, ay nagpapanatili ng mga measurement statistics na iyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"For each $R_j$, prepare an ancilla in $|+\\rangle$, apply its controlled-Pauli factors and any sign correction, then measure in the $X$ basis (H followed by a computational-basis measurement). The ideal result is bit `0`, corresponding to eigenvalue $+1$.\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Para sa bawat $R_j$, ihanda ang isang ancilla sa $|+\\rangle$, ilapat ang mga controlled-Pauli factor nito at anumang sign correction, saka sukatin sa $X$ basis (H na sinusundan ng computational-basis measurement). Ang ideal na resulta ay bit na `0`, na tumutugma sa eigenvalue na $+1$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The next cell measures all 20 generators to illustrate the routing cost; using a subset also gives valid right-only checks. The 20 data qubits and 20 ancillas sit on one line, as on a device with only nearest-neighbor couplers. Each ancilla must meet every data qubit in the support of its $R_j$, so the ancillas are swapped through the data line. A controlled-Pauli fused with the swap that follows it costs two CZ gates [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), and a swap past a data qubit outside the support still costs three. The cell checks that no ancilla fires without noise, compares CZ counts, and draws the circuit. The payload sits left of the barrier, and the diamond of SWAP gates carries the ancillas up through the data line.\n"
msgstr "Sinusukat ng susunod na cell ang lahat ng 20 generator para ilarawan ang gastos sa routing; ang paggamit ng subset ay nagbibigay din ng wastong right-only check. Ang 20 data qubit at 20 ancilla ay nasa iisang linya, gaya ng sa device na nearest-neighbor coupler lang ang mayroon. Kailangang makaharap ng bawat ancilla ang bawat data qubit sa support ng $R_j$ nito, kaya ang mga ancilla ay sina-swap sa data line. Ang controlled-Pauli na pinagsama sa kasunod na swap ay nagkakahalaga ng dalawang CZ gate [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), at ang swap sa data qubit na wala sa support ay nagkakahalaga pa rin ng tatlo. Tinitiyak ng cell na walang ancilla na nag-fire kapag walang ingay, inihahambing ang bilang ng CZ, at iginuguhit ang circuit. Ang payload ay nasa kaliwa ng barrier, at ang diamond ng mga SWAP gate ang nagdadala sa mga ancilla pataas sa data line.\n"

