msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/4c2e2a1e-1.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every check in this circuit is valid, and each ancilla measures one stabilizer, so each catches the faults that anti-commute with its $R_j$. The cost is the problem. In this layout, reaching the supports takes 400 swaps, and even with each controlled-Pauli fused into a swap, the checks need more than twenty times as many CZ gates as the payload they protect. Those gates and the waiting ancillas add errors of their own, so on hardware the added noise can easily outweigh what the checks detect. These counts describe this routing construction, not a minimum: placement and check support determine the routing cost on the nearest-neighbor line.\n"
"A check need not occupy one time slice. A *wire* $w$ is a qubit between two layers. Let $A_w$ be the circuit prefix up to that wire. The Pauli's back-propagator is $B(P,w)=A_w^\\dagger P A_w$. For input $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, couplings in time order $1,\\ldots,m$ form a valid check when:\n"
msgstr ""
"Wasto ang bawat check sa circuit na ito, at sinusukat ng bawat ancilla ang isang stabilizer, kaya nahuhuli ng bawat isa ang mga fault na nag-a-anti-commute sa $R_j$ nito. Ang gastos ang problema. Sa layout na ito, 400 swap ang kailangan para maabot ang mga support, at kahit na may bawat controlled-Pauli na pinagsama sa swap, ang mga check ay nangangailangan ng mahigit dalawampung beses na mas maraming CZ gate kaysa sa payload na pinoprotektahan nila. Ang mga gate na iyon at ang naghihintay na mga ancilla ay nagdaragdag ng sarili nilang mga error, kaya sa hardware ay madaling lampasan ng dagdag na ingay ang natutukoy ng mga check. Inilalarawan ng mga bilang na ito ang routing construction na ito, hindi ang minimum: ang placement at check support ang tumutukoy sa gastos sa routing sa nearest-neighbor na linya.\n"
"Ang isang check ay hindi kailangang sumakop sa iisang time slice. Ang *wire* na $w$ ay isang qubit sa pagitan ng dalawang layer. Hayaang ang $A_w$ ay ang circuit prefix hanggang sa wire na iyon. Ang back-propagator ng Pauli ay $B(P,w)=A_w^\\dagger P A_w$. Para sa input na $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, ang mga coupling sa pagkakasunod-sunod ng oras na $1,\\ldots,m$ ay bumubuo ng wastong check kapag:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$B(P_m,w_m)\\cdots B(P_1,w_1)=i^\\phi S,\\qquad S\\in\\langle Z_0,\\ldots,Z_{n-1}\\rangle.$$\n"
msgstr "$$B(P_m,w_m)\\cdots B(P_1,w_1)=i^\\phi S,\\qquad S\\in\\langle Z_0,\\ldots,Z_{n-1}\\rangle.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prepare the ancilla in $|+\\rangle$, apply each controlled Pauli at its wire, cancel the accumulated phase $i^\\phi$ on the ancilla, and measure it in the $X$ basis ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Eq. 2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)). We keep every coupling on one neighboring data qubit to avoid routing while allowing support at different times.\n"
"<span id=\"paulice\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Ihanda ang ancilla sa $|+\\rangle$, ilapat ang bawat controlled Pauli sa wire nito, kanselahin ang naipong phase na $i^\\phi$ sa ancilla, at sukatin ito sa $X$ basis ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Eq. 2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)). Pinananatili natin ang bawat coupling sa iisang kalapit na data qubit para iwasan ang routing habang pinapayagan ang support sa iba't ibang oras.\n"
"<span id=\"paulice\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.4 Spacetime checks with Paulice {#24-spacetime-checks-with-paulice}"
msgstr "2.4 Mga spacetime check gamit ang Paulice {#24-spacetime-checks-with-paulice}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The right-only example in Section 2.3.3 showed that removing $L$ does not eliminate routing. Each ancilla still has to reach the support of its output stabilizer. The local spacetime construction introduced above avoids this routing by placing an ancilla's couplings at different times on a single neighboring data wire.\n"
msgstr "Ipinakita ng right-only na halimbawa sa Section 2.3.3 na hindi inaalis ng pag-alis ng $L$ ang routing. Kailangan pa ring maabot ng bawat ancilla ang support ng output stabilizer nito. Iniiwasan ng lokal na spacetime construction na ipinakilala sa itaas ang routing na ito sa pamamagitan ng paglalagay ng mga coupling ng ancilla sa iba't ibang oras sa iisang kalapit na data wire.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Qiskit Paulice](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-paulice) automates this search for a given circuit and measurement. Each data qubit with a free neighbor on the device can be a *target*, and that neighbor becomes its check ancilla. With `method=\"windowed\"`, as in the [IBM Quantum spacetime-code tutorial](/tutorials/spacetime-codes), `add_pauli_checks` searches windows of couplings along each target's wire. A check's syndrome is the XOR of its ancilla bit and, where the search needs them, final data bits listed in `check_support`. Data bits are read out anyway, so that part of a check costs no gates.\n"
msgstr "Ino-automate ng [Qiskit Paulice](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-paulice) ang paghahanap na ito para sa isang circuit at measurement. Ang bawat data qubit na may libreng kapitbahay sa device ay maaaring maging *target*, at ang kapitbahay na iyon ang nagiging check ancilla nito. Sa `method=\"windowed\"`, gaya sa [IBM Quantum spacetime-code tutorial](/tutorials/spacetime-codes), hinahanap ng `add_pauli_checks` ang mga window ng coupling sa wire ng bawat target. Ang syndrome ng isang check ay ang XOR ng ancilla bit nito at, kung kailangan ng paghahanap, ng mga final data bit na nakalista sa `check_support`. Binabasa pa rin ang mga data bit, kaya walang gate na nagagastos para sa bahaging iyon ng check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paulice scores checks with *gamma*: under a noise model built from the device calibration, it estimates the quasi-probability cost of cancelling the noise the checks leave undetected within the lightcone of the measurement, and lower is better. Gamma is a model score, distinct from the acceptance overhead $1/A$ of Part 1. Checks are committed one target at a time, and the search returns the circuit after each commitment. The model takes the error of each data-line coupler from the calibration but gives the couplers a check adds a typical rate, so it would not notice a faulty one; we therefore keep only target-ancilla pairs with calibrated coupler error below 0.05 and ancilla readout error below 0.1.\n"
msgstr "Binibigyan ng iskor ng Paulice ang mga check gamit ang *gamma*: sa ilalim ng noise model na binuo mula sa device calibration, tinatantya nito ang quasi-probability cost ng pagkansela sa ingay na hindi natutukoy ng mga check sa loob ng lightcone ng measurement, at mas mabuti ang mas mababa. Ang Gamma ay model score, na iba sa acceptance overhead na $1/A$ ng Part 1. Isa-isang kino-commit ang mga check kada target, at ibinabalik ng paghahanap ang circuit pagkatapos ng bawat commitment. Kinukuha ng model ang error ng bawat coupler ng data-line mula sa calibration pero nagbibigay ito ng karaniwang rate sa mga coupler na idinagdag ng check, kaya hindi nito mapapansin ang may sira; kaya pinapanatili lang natin ang mga target-ancilla pair na may calibrated coupler error na mas mababa sa 0.05 at ancilla readout error na mas mababa sa 0.1.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment), we extend the same 20-qubit circuit construction from five to ten CZ layers, keeping the generator and random seed fixed. The deeper payload gives errors more opportunities to accumulate and spread, so that we can test whether post-selection can outweigh the added check cost. We run this payload forward, without its inverse, and sample ten signed stabilizers of its ideal output. The stabilizers and Paulice checks are computed for this deeper circuit. We select one physical data line and reuse it for every unchecked and checked circuit. The first sampled stabilizer $G_0$ supplies the measurement for the placement comparison below, and [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment) repeats the search for each stabilizer. We first recall how a check's support moves backward through the gates.\n"
"The idea is to take a check operator and pull it backward through the circuit, one gate at a time. At each layer, we record which qubits the operator touches; together, these qubits and layers form the check's detection region. To do this, we need to know how Pauli operators transform when they pass through a gate in reverse. For any Clifford gate, the result is always another Pauli, which is what makes the tracking exact. Our payload uses CZ gates and single-qubit Clifford gates. A CZ, for example, maps:\n"
msgstr ""
"Para sa Seksyon [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment), pinalawig namin ang parehong 20-qubit circuit construction mula lima hanggang sampung CZ layer, habang pinananatiling nakapirmi ang generator at random seed. Ang mas malalim na payload ay nagbibigay sa mga error ng mas maraming pagkakataong mag-ipon at kumalat, para masubukan namin kung kaya bang higitan ng post-selection ang dagdag na gastos ng mga check. Pinatatakbo namin ang payload na ito pasulong, nang walang inverse nito, at nagsa-sample kami ng sampung signed stabilizer ng ideal na output nito. Kinukwenta ang mga stabilizer at Paulice check para sa mas malalim na circuit na ito. Pumipili kami ng isang pisikal na data line at ginagamit ito ulit para sa bawat unchecked at checked na circuit. Ang unang na-sample na stabilizer na $G_0$ ang nagbibigay ng measurement para sa paghahambing ng placement sa ibaba, at inuulit ng [Seksyon 2.5](#hardware-experiment) ang paghahanap para sa bawat stabilizer. Una nating balikan kung paano gumagalaw pabalik ang support ng isang check sa mga gate.\n"
"Ang ideya ay kumuha ng check operator at hilahin ito pabalik sa circuit, isang gate bawat hakbang. Sa bawat layer, itinatala natin kung aling mga qubit ang hinahawakan ng operator; ang mga qubit at layer na ito ay bumubuo ng detection region ng check. Para magawa ito, kailangan nating malaman kung paano nagbabago ang mga Pauli operator kapag dumaan sila nang pabalik sa isang gate. Para sa anumang Clifford gate, ang resulta ay palaging isa pang Pauli, kaya nagiging eksakto ang pagsubaybay. Gumagamit ang ating payload ng mga CZ gate at single-qubit Clifford gate. Halimbawa, ang CZ ay nagma-map ng:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$X_a \\xrightarrow{\\text{CZ}(a, b)} X_a Z_b,$$\n"
msgstr "$$X_a \\xrightarrow{\\text{CZ}(a, b)} X_a Z_b,$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "so an $X$ in a check's support picks up a $Z$ on the partner qubit, while a $Z$ passes through unchanged. Each two-qubit layer can add qubits to the support or remove them, so the detection region changes shape as it moves backward in time. The rules for CNOT and CZ are collected below.\n"
msgstr "kaya ang isang $X$ sa support ng isang check ay nagkakaroon ng $Z$ sa partner na qubit, habang ang $Z$ ay dumadaan nang walang pagbabago. Ang bawat two-qubit layer ay maaaring magdagdag o mag-alis ng mga qubit sa support, kaya nagbabago ang hugis ng detection region habang gumagalaw ito pabalik sa oras. Nakalap sa ibaba ang mga patakaran para sa CNOT at CZ.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Conjugation rules for two-qubit gates, and what single-qubit gates do\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Mga conjugation rule para sa two-qubit gate, at ang ginagawa ng single-qubit gate\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The conjugation $U^\\dagger P U$ pulls a check operator $P$ at the output of a gate back to its input. Forward propagation of an error instead uses $U P U^\\dagger$. For Clifford gates, the result is always another Pauli.\n"
msgstr "Ang conjugation na $U^\\dagger P U$ ay humihila ng check operator na $P$ sa output ng isang gate pabalik sa input nito. Ang forward propagation ng isang error ay gumagamit naman ng $U P U^\\dagger$. Para sa mga Clifford gate, ang resulta ay palaging isa pang Pauli.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Single-qubit gates (H, S, and so on) can change the type of error on a wire (for example, $X \\leftrightarrow Z$ through H) but cannot spread it to another qubit. Only two-qubit entangling gates move errors between wires, which is why detection regions grow only at entangling layers.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga single-qubit gate (H, S, at iba pa) ay maaaring magpalit ng uri ng error sa isang wire (halimbawa, $X \\leftrightarrow Z$ sa pamamagitan ng H) pero hindi nila ito maikakalat sa ibang qubit. Mga two-qubit entangling gate lang ang naglilipat ng mga error sa pagitan ng mga wire, kaya lumalaki lang ang mga detection region sa mga entangling layer.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*CNOT* (control $c$, target $t$), an *asymmetric* gate:\n"
msgstr "*CNOT* (control $c$, target $t$), isang *asymmetric* na gate:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t, \\quad Z_c \\mapsto Z_c, \\quad X_t \\mapsto X_t, \\quad Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t.$$\n"
msgstr "$$X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t, \\quad Z_c \\mapsto Z_c, \\quad X_t \\mapsto X_t, \\quad Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "CNOT does \"if control is 1, flip target.\" This asymmetry determines how different errors propagate:\n"
msgstr "Ginagawa ng CNOT ang \"kung 1 ang control, i-flip ang target.\" Ang asymmetry na ito ang nagtatakda kung paano kumakalat ang iba't ibang error:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bit-flips ($X$) travel *control → target*. An $X$ on the control changes whether the target gets flipped, so the error spreads to the target ($X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t$). An $X$ on the target flips it regardless of the control, so it stays put ($X_t \\mapsto X_t$).\n"
msgstr "Ang mga bit-flip ($X$) ay naglalakbay *control → target*. Binabago ng $X$ sa control kung mai-flip ang target, kaya kumakalat ang error sa target ($X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t$). Ang $X$ sa target ay nagfi-flip nito anuman ang control, kaya nananatili ito sa kinalalagyan ($X_t \\mapsto X_t$).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Phase-flips ($Z$) travel *target → control*. CNOT makes the target's value depend on the control ($|c,t\\rangle \\to |c, c \\oplus t\\rangle$), so a phase applied to the target effectively becomes conditional on the control's value, kicking a phase back to the control. This is the same *phase kickback* mechanism used in algorithms like Deutsch-Jozsa and quantum phase estimation. The result is $Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t$. Meanwhile $Z$ on the control does not involve the target at all, so $Z_c \\mapsto Z_c$.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga phase-flip ($Z$) ay naglalakbay *target → control*. Ginagawa ng CNOT na umasa ang halaga ng target sa control ($|c,t\\rangle \\to |c, c \\oplus t\\rangle$), kaya ang phase na inilapat sa target ay nagiging conditional sa halaga ng control, at ibinabalik ang phase sa control. Ito ang parehong mekanismong *phase kickback* na ginagamit sa mga algorithm tulad ng Deutsch-Jozsa at quantum phase estimation. Ang resulta ay $Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t$. Samantala, ang $Z$ sa control ay hindi sangkot ang target, kaya $Z_c \\mapsto Z_c$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In short: *bit-flips flow forward* (control → target), *phase-flips flow backward* (target → control). This directional asymmetry is why backward propagation of $Z$-type checks through a CNOT chain fans out from target toward controls.\n"
msgstr "Sa madaling sabi: *pasulong ang daloy ng mga bit-flip* (control → target), *paatras ang daloy ng mga phase-flip* (target → control). Ang directional asymmetry na ito ang dahilan kung bakit ang paatras na propagation ng mga $Z$-type check sa isang CNOT chain ay kumakalat mula sa target papunta sa mga control.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*CZ* (qubits $a$, $b$), a *symmetric* gate:\n"
msgstr "*CZ* (mga qubit $a$, $b$), isang *symmetric* na gate:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$X_a \\mapsto X_a Z_b, \\quad Z_a \\mapsto Z_a, \\quad X_b \\mapsto Z_a X_b, \\quad Z_b \\mapsto Z_b.$$\n"
msgstr "$$X_a \\mapsto X_a Z_b, \\quad Z_a \\mapsto Z_a, \\quad X_b \\mapsto Z_a X_b, \\quad Z_b \\mapsto Z_b.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unlike CNOT, CZ treats both qubits identically; there is no \"control\" or \"target.\" A bit-flip on either qubit picks up a phase-flip on the other. Phase-flips pass through unchanged on both sides. The symmetry means error propagation looks the same from either direction.\n"
msgstr "Hindi tulad ng CNOT, parehong tinatrato ng CZ ang dalawang qubit; walang \"control\" o \"target.\" Ang bit-flip sa alinmang qubit ay nagkakaroon ng phase-flip sa isa pa. Ang mga phase-flip ay dumadaan nang walang pagbabago sa magkabilang panig. Ibig sabihin ng symmetry, pareho ang itsura ng error propagation mula sa alinmang direksyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The pattern to remember: entangling gates *spread* errors. A single-qubit error before the gate can become a two-qubit error after it. This is both the source of the problem (errors propagate through the circuit) and the basis of detection (a check's sensitivity propagates the same way).\n"
msgstr "Ang pattern na dapat tandaan: ang mga entangling gate ay *nagkakalat* ng mga error. Ang isang single-qubit error bago ang gate ay maaaring maging two-qubit error pagkatapos nito. Ito ang pinagmulan ng problema (kumakalat ang mga error sa circuit) at ang batayan ng detection (kumakalat din sa parehong paraan ang sensitivity ng isang check).\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/bed76e8d-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/bed76e8d-1.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first table lists one check per target. Each check couples its ancilla to the target wire at a few points along the payload, the layers shown in the table, and its syndrome might add final data bits. The second table follows the search as it commits checks. Compare the change in gamma and the added two-qubit gates at each step. The targets are ordered by their single-check scores, but overlapping detection regions and noise from added gates mean that the largest improvement need not come first, and a committed check can raise gamma. The device map shows data qubits in green, committed check ancillas in orange, and unused qubits in gray. Each check ancilla is next to its target, so the couplings need no routing.\n"
"Each check covers the spacetime region its couplings and syndrome bits reach by back-propagation, as in Section 2.3.3. Coverage is not the whole story, however. Each check adds gates, and gamma weighs the detected noise against the noise those gates bring. Gamma depends on the noise model and on the chosen measurement, so a lower gamma is a search preference, not a guarantee of a better hardware result, and it is not an acceptance rate or a measured fidelity.\n"
msgstr ""
"Inililista ng unang talahanayan ang isang check bawat target. Ikinakabit ng bawat check ang ancilla nito sa target wire sa ilang punto sa payload, ang mga layer na nakasaad sa talahanayan, at ang syndrome nito ay maaaring magdagdag ng mga huling data bit. Sinusundan ng ikalawang talahanayan ang paghahanap habang nagko-commit ito ng mga check. Ihambing ang pagbabago sa gamma at ang mga idinagdag na two-qubit gate sa bawat hakbang. Nakaayos ang mga target ayon sa kanilang single-check score, pero dahil sa magkakapatong na detection region at ingay mula sa mga idinagdag na gate, hindi kailangang mauna ang pinakamalaking pagbuti, at ang isang na-commit na check ay maaaring magpataas ng gamma. Ipinapakita ng device map ang mga data qubit sa berde, ang mga na-commit na check ancilla sa orange, at ang mga hindi nagamit na qubit sa gray. Katabi ng target nito ang bawat check ancilla, kaya hindi na kailangan ng routing ang mga coupling.\n"
"Sinasaklaw ng bawat check ang spacetime region na naaabot ng mga coupling at syndrome bit nito sa pamamagitan ng back-propagation, tulad sa Seksyon 2.3.3. Pero hindi lang ito ang kabuuan ng kuwento. Nagdaragdag ng mga gate ang bawat check, at tinitimbang ng gamma ang natukoy na ingay laban sa ingay na dala ng mga gate na iyon. Nakadepende ang gamma sa noise model at sa piniling measurement, kaya ang mas mababang gamma ay kagustuhan sa paghahanap, hindi garantiya ng mas magandang resulta sa hardware, at hindi ito acceptance rate o nasukat na fidelity.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The checks also depend on the measured observable. Here they were chosen for $G_0$. [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment) repeats the search for every sampled stabilizer and runs the resulting circuits on hardware.\n"
msgstr "Nakadepende rin ang mga check sa sinusukat na observable. Dito, pinili ang mga ito para sa $G_0$. Inuulit ng [Seksyon 2.5](#hardware-experiment) ang paghahanap para sa bawat na-sample na stabilizer at pinatatakbo ang mga nagreresultang circuit sa hardware.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-7}"
msgstr "Suriin ang iyong pag-unawa {#check-your-understanding-7}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A check has a detection region covering some set of spacetime locations. Does this mean it catches every error at those locations?\n"
msgstr "May detection region ang isang check na sumasaklaw sa isang set ng mga spacetime location. Ibig bang sabihin nito ay nahuhuli nito ang bawat error sa mga lokasyong iyon?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not every type. At each location in the detection region, the check detects errors that anti-commute with its propagated support there; errors that commute pass through undetected. For one check, the propagated support at a location is a single Pauli, so the check catches two of $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ there and misses the third. This is why a single check has weaknesses even within its own detection region, and why complementary checks with different Pauli types or placements are needed for broader protection.\n"
msgstr "Hindi lahat ng uri. Sa bawat lokasyon sa detection region, natutukoy ng check ang mga error na nag-a-anti-commute sa propagated support nito doon; ang mga error na nagko-commute ay dumadaan nang hindi natutukoy. Para sa isang check, ang propagated support sa isang lokasyon ay isang Pauli, kaya nahuhuli ng check ang dalawa sa $X$, $Y$, at $Z$ doon at hindi nito nahuhuli ang pangatlo. Ito ang dahilan kung bakit may mga kahinaan ang isang check kahit sa sarili nitong detection region, at kung bakit kailangan ang mga komplementaryong check na may magkakaibang uri ng Pauli o placement para sa mas malawak na proteksyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"<span id=\"hardware-experiment\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.5 Hardware experiment: spacetime checks on the payload {#25-hardware-experiment-spacetime-checks-on-the-payload}"
msgstr "2.5 Hardware experiment: mga spacetime check sa payload {#25-hardware-experiment-spacetime-checks-on-the-payload}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Section 2.4](#paulice) used a device-based noise model to select and score checks. We now test on hardware whether post-selection brings the measured observables closer to their ideal values, and how many shots that improvement costs. Using the same ten-layer payload and fixed data line, we compare an unchecked baseline with an increasing number of checks. Following the [spacetime-code tutorial](/tutorials/spacetime-codes), we repeat the search for each sampled stabilizer and compare raw and post-selected expectation values alongside acceptance.\n"
msgstr "Gumamit ang [Seksyon 2.4](#paulice) ng device-based na noise model para pumili at mag-score ng mga check. Ngayon, sinusubukan namin sa hardware kung inilalapit ng post-selection ang mga nasukat na observable sa kanilang mga ideal na halaga, at kung ilang shot ang halaga ng pagbuting iyon. Gamit ang parehong sampung-layer na payload at nakapirming data line, inihahambing namin ang isang unchecked na baseline sa dumaraming bilang ng mga check. Kasunod ng [spacetime-code tutorial](/tutorials/spacetime-codes), inuulit namin ang paghahanap para sa bawat na-sample na stabilizer at inihahambing ang raw at post-selected na expectation value kasama ang acceptance.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why sampled stabilizers? {#why-sampled-stabilizers}"
msgstr "Bakit mga na-sample na stabilizer? {#why-sampled-stabilizers}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The payload prepares a stabilizer state $|\\psi\\rangle = U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, and its projector is the average of the $2^n$ signed elements of its stabilizer group, $|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi| = 2^{-n} \\sum_G G$. The fidelity $\\langle\\psi|\\rho|\\psi\\rangle$ of a prepared state $\\rho$ is therefore the average of $\\langle G\\rangle_\\rho$ over a uniformly random stabilizer $G$, and every $G$ has ideal value $+1$. Measuring one $G$ needs only single-qubit rotations before readout, and its value in each shot is a sign times the parity of the measured bits on its support. A few sampled stabilizers give an estimate of the fidelity whose uncertainty shrinks with the number of samples, so the benchmark stays verifiable at any qubit count. Because the sampled stabilizers carry $X$ and $Y$ factors as well as $Z$, the estimate responds to phase errors as well as bit flips.\n"
msgstr "Naghahanda ang payload ng isang stabilizer state na $|\\psi\\rangle = U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, at ang projector nito ay ang average ng $2^n$ signed na elemento ng stabilizer group nito, $|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi| = 2^{-n} \\sum_G G$. Ang fidelity na $\\langle\\psi|\\rho|\\psi\\rangle$ ng isang inihandang state na $\\rho$ ay samakatuwid ang average ng $\\langle G\\rangle_\\rho$ sa isang uniformly random na stabilizer na $G$, at ang bawat $G$ ay may ideal na halagang $+1$. Ang pagsukat ng isang $G$ ay nangangailangan lang ng single-qubit rotation bago ang readout, at ang halaga nito sa bawat shot ay isang sign na pinarami sa parity ng mga nasukat na bit sa support nito. Ang ilang na-sample na stabilizer ay nagbibigay ng tantiya ng fidelity na ang uncertainty ay lumiliit habang dumarami ang mga sample, kaya nananatiling verifiable ang benchmark sa anumang bilang ng qubit. Dahil ang mga na-sample na stabilizer ay may mga factor na $X$ at $Y$ pati na $Z$, tumutugon ang tantiya sa mga phase error at gayundin sa mga bit flip.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"A note on the word &quot;fidelity&quot; in this section\">\n"
msgstr "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Paalala tungkol sa salitang &quot;fidelity&quot; sa seksyong ito\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For the bare circuits (B0), the average of $\\langle G\\rangle$ over the sampled stabilizers estimates the fidelity of the prepared state. Ten samples give a rough estimate, whose uncertainty depends on both shot noise and variation between the sampled stabilizers. For the checked layouts it does not, even before post-selection. Their checks are chosen separately for every stabilizer, so the data need not be in the same state across the measurement settings. A check can preserve the ideal measurement statistics without preserving the full data state, as noted for the one-sided example. We therefore read both the raw and the post-selected averages of the checked layouts as benchmarks of expectation values, observable by observable, and not as the fidelity of one state.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"Para sa mga bare circuit (B0), ang average ng $\\langle G\\rangle$ sa mga na-sample na stabilizer ay tumatantiya sa fidelity ng inihandang state. Ang sampung sample ay nagbibigay ng magaspang na tantiya, na ang uncertainty ay nakadepende sa shot noise at sa pagkakaiba-iba ng mga na-sample na stabilizer. Para sa mga checked na layout, hindi ito totoo, kahit bago pa ang post-selection. Magkakahiwalay na pinipili ang mga check nila para sa bawat stabilizer, kaya hindi kailangang nasa parehong state ang data sa lahat ng measurement setting. Maaaring panatilihin ng isang check ang ideal na measurement statistics nang hindi pinapanatili ang buong data state, tulad ng nabanggit sa one-sided na halimbawa. Kaya binabasa namin ang raw at post-selected na average ng mga checked na layout bilang mga benchmark ng expectation value, observable por observable, at hindi bilang fidelity ng iisang state.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To test how the number of checks affects detection performance, we compare a ladder of check counts on every sampled stabilizer. All layouts share the payload, the data line, and the measurement, and they differ only in how many checks they add.\n"
msgstr "Para masubukan kung paano nakakaapekto ang bilang ng mga check sa detection performance, inihahambing namin ang isang ladder ng mga bilang ng check sa bawat na-sample na stabilizer. Pareho ang payload, data line, at measurement ng lahat ng layout, at ang pagkakaiba lang nila ay kung ilang check ang idinadagdag nila.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.5.1 The layouts {#251-the-layouts}"
msgstr "2.5.1 Ang mga layout {#251-the-layouts}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For every sampled stabilizer $G$, `add_pauli_checks` runs the search of [Section 2.4](#paulice) on the circuit that measures $G$, with the targets in the order found there for $G_0$, from the best single check down. It commits one check per target and returns the circuit after each commitment, so one call per stabilizer gives the whole ladder. The important question is how many committed checks to keep.\n"
msgstr "Para sa bawat na-sample na stabilizer na $G$, pinatatakbo ng `add_pauli_checks` ang paghahanap ng [Seksyon 2.4](#paulice) sa circuit na sumusukat sa $G$, kasama ang mga target sa pagkakasunod-sunod na natagpuan doon para sa $G_0$, mula sa pinakamahusay na single check pababa. Nagko-commit ito ng isang check bawat target at ibinabalik ang circuit pagkatapos ng bawat commitment, kaya ang isang tawag bawat stabilizer ay nagbibigay ng buong ladder. Ang mahalagang tanong ay kung ilang na-commit na check ang itatago.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The layouts are B0, P1, P2, P4, and the last P layout: zero, one, two, and four committed checks, and all of them. The name gives the check count. The last layout keeps every check the search commits, as the tutorial does, and its count depends on how many data qubits have a usable free neighbor. Reading the ladder in order compares successive check-augmented implementations, with B0, the unchecked circuit returned by the search, as the control.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga layout ay B0, P1, P2, P4, at ang huling P layout: zero, isa, dalawa, at apat na na-commit na check, at lahat ng mga ito. Ang pangalan ang nagsasabi ng bilang ng check. Itinatago ng huling layout ang bawat check na kino-commit ng paghahanap, tulad ng ginagawa ng tutorial, at ang bilang nito ay nakadepende sa kung ilang data qubit ang may magagamit na libreng kapitbahay. Ang pagbasa sa ladder nang magkakasunod ay naghahambing ng magkakasunod na check-augmented na implementasyon, kung saan ang B0, ang unchecked na circuit na ibinalik ng paghahanap, ang control.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each $G$ gets its own checks, because the search depends on the measured observable. The selected data line, committed checks, gate counts, and model scores depend on the backend and its calibration. Use the tables generated by the current run as the reference, and compare the hardware results within that run.\n"
msgstr "Ang bawat $G$ ay may sariling mga check, dahil nakadepende ang paghahanap sa sinusukat na observable. Ang napiling data line, mga na-commit na check, bilang ng gate, at model score ay nakadepende sa backend at sa calibration nito. Gamitin bilang reference ang mga talahanayang nabuo ng kasalukuyang run, at ihambing ang mga resulta sa hardware sa loob ng run na iyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Availability note\">\n"
msgstr "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Paalala sa availability\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On a data line with few usable free neighbors, the search commits fewer than four checks. P4 is then dropped and the last P layout keeps the checks as they are. If one stabilizer commits fewer checks than the others, the ladder stops at that smaller count for all of them.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa isang data line na kakaunti ang magagamit na libreng kapitbahay, mas kaunti sa apat na check ang kino-commit ng paghahanap. Aalisin na ang P4 at itatago ng huling P layout ang mga check kung ano sila. Kung ang isang stabilizer ay nagko-commit ng mas kaunting check kaysa sa iba, hihinto ang ladder sa mas maliit na bilang na iyon para sa lahat ng mga ito.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.5.2 Comparing layouts without running {#252-comparing-layouts-without-running}"
msgstr "2.5.2 Paghahambing ng mga layout nang hindi nagpapatakbo {#252-comparing-layouts-without-running}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The table compares the layouts on two quantities available before any shot is taken, both averaged over the stabilizers. Compare gamma at each step with B0 and the preceding layout. A lower value indicates less uncovered noise under the model, and neither a monotonic decrease nor the highest value at B0 is guaranteed. The added two-qubit gates show the price. Each check couples its ancilla to its target at a few points, and because the ancilla is a neighbor of the target, no routing is needed.\n"
msgstr "Inihahambing ng talahanayan ang mga layout sa dalawang dami na available bago pa kumuha ng anumang shot, na parehong naka-average sa mga stabilizer. Ihambing ang gamma sa bawat hakbang sa B0 at sa nauunang layout. Ang mas mababang halaga ay nagpapahiwatig ng mas kaunting hindi nasaklaw na ingay sa ilalim ng modelo, at hindi garantisado ang monotonic na pagbaba o ang pinakamataas na halaga sa B0. Ipinapakita ng mga idinagdag na two-qubit gate ang presyo. Ikinakabit ng bawat check ang ancilla nito sa target nito sa ilang punto, at dahil kapitbahay ng target ang ancilla, hindi kailangan ng routing.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The addon models device noise as a sparse Pauli-Lindblad channel: a set of Pauli fault generators $P_k$, one per gate location and Pauli type, each with a rate $\\lambda_k$. For a two-qubit gate with calibrated error probability $p$, the error is split evenly over the 15 non-identity two-qubit Paulis, and each generator gets $\\lambda = -\\tfrac{1}{2}\\ln(1 - 2p/15)$. Propagating operators through the circuit sorts the generators into three groups: those that commute with every measured operator (harmless, since they cannot change the output), those that anti-commute with some check's propagated support (detected, since they flip a syndrome bit), and the remainder, the uncovered faults within the measurement lightcone. Gamma is:\n"
msgstr "Minomodelo ng addon ang device noise bilang sparse Pauli-Lindblad channel: isang set ng mga Pauli fault generator na $P_k$, isa bawat gate location at uri ng Pauli, bawat isa ay may rate na $\\lambda_k$. Para sa isang two-qubit gate na may calibrated na error probability na $p$, hinahati nang pantay ang error sa 15 non-identity na two-qubit Pauli, at bawat generator ay nakakakuha ng $\\lambda = -\\tfrac{1}{2}\\ln(1 - 2p/15)$. Ang pag-propagate ng mga operator sa circuit ay nag-uuri sa mga generator sa tatlong grupo: yaong nagko-commute sa bawat nasukat na operator (hindi nakakapinsala, dahil hindi nila mababago ang output), yaong nag-a-anti-commute sa propagated support ng ilang check (natukoy, dahil nagfi-flip sila ng syndrome bit), at ang natitira, ang mga hindi nasaklaw na fault sa loob ng measurement lightcone. Ang gamma ay:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$\\gamma = \\exp\\Big(2 \\sum_{k\\,\\in\\,\\text{uncovered}} \\lambda_k\\Big),$$\n"
msgstr "$$\\gamma = \\exp\\Big(2 \\sum_{k\\,\\in\\,\\text{uncovered}} \\lambda_k\\Big),$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "the quasi-probability cost of the channel that would invert exactly that residual noise ([van den Berg, Minev, Kandala, and Temme, Nat. Phys. 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.09866)). For this convention, the usual PEC sampling cost scales as $\\gamma^2$, while post-selection has a separate acceptance cost $1/A$. $\\gamma = 1$ means no positive-rate generator remains uncovered within the measurement lightcone. Committing a check that covers generators of total rate $\\Lambda$ divides gamma by $e^{2\\Lambda}$, while the generators of the check's own gates that it leaves undetected multiply gamma back up. This multiplicative structure is why each well-placed check lowers gamma by its own factor, and why a check that adds more noise than it covers can raise it.\n"
msgstr "ang quasi-probability cost ng channel na eksaktong magbabaligtad sa natitirang ingay na iyon ([van den Berg, Minev, Kandala, at Temme, Nat. Phys. 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.09866)). Sa convention na ito, ang karaniwang PEC sampling cost ay lumalaki bilang $\\gamma^2$, habang ang post-selection ay may hiwalay na acceptance cost na $1/A$. Ang $\\gamma = 1$ ay nangangahulugang walang positive-rate na generator na nananatiling hindi nasasaklaw sa loob ng measurement lightcone. Ang pag-commit ng check na sumasaklaw sa mga generator na may kabuuang rate na $\\Lambda$ ay naghahati sa gamma ng $e^{2\\Lambda}$, habang ang mga generator ng sariling gate ng check na hindi nito natutukoy ay nagpaparami ulit sa gamma. Ang multiplicative na istrukturang ito ang dahilan kung bakit ibinababa ng bawat maayos na nailagay na check ang gamma ayon sa sarili nitong factor, at kung bakit ang check na nagdaragdag ng mas maraming ingay kaysa sa nasasaklaw nito ay maaaring magpataas nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Because the exponent accumulates hundreds of generator rates, absolute gamma is exponentially sensitive to the calibration scale. A modest change in the per-gate error rate raises gamma to a power, so values can differ substantially between backends and between calibration days. Compare gammas only within a single run on a single backend, where the ordering is what matters. When a linear, additive quantity is needed, use $\\ln\\gamma / 2 = \\sum_k \\lambda_k$, the uncovered rate budget; each check's contribution then reads off as a difference of $\\ln\\gamma$.\n"
msgstr "Dahil ang exponent ay nag-iipon ng daan-daang generator rate, ang absolute na gamma ay exponentially sensitive sa calibration scale. Ang katamtamang pagbabago sa per-gate error rate ay nagtataas ng gamma sa isang power, kaya ang mga halaga ay maaaring magkaiba nang malaki sa pagitan ng mga backend at ng mga araw ng calibration. Ihambing lang ang mga gamma sa loob ng iisang run sa iisang backend, kung saan ang pagkakasunod-sunod ang mahalaga. Kapag kailangan ng linear, additive na dami, gamitin ang $\\ln\\gamma / 2 = \\sum_k \\lambda_k$, ang hindi nasaklawang rate budget; ang kontribusyon ng bawat check ay mababasa bilang pagkakaiba ng $\\ln\\gamma$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The noise model comes from `PauliceNoiseModel.from_backend(backend, data_line)`. Each data-line coupler keeps its own calibrated error, readout enters as the average over the data line, and the couplers a check adds get a typical rate, which is why [Section 2.4](#paulice) screens the actual ancilla couplers against the calibration.\n"
msgstr "Ang noise model ay nagmumula sa `PauliceNoiseModel.from_backend(backend, data_line)`. Pinapanatili ng bawat data-line coupler ang sarili nitong calibrated na error, ang readout ay pumapasok bilang average sa data line, at ang mga coupler na idinagdag ng isang check ay nakakakuha ng karaniwang rate, kaya sinusuri ng [Seksyon 2.4](#paulice) ang aktwal na ancilla coupler laban sa calibration.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.5.3 Hardware results {#253-hardware-results}"
msgstr "2.5.3 Mga resulta sa hardware {#253-hardware-results}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The addon inserts its checks on the transpiled circuit, so it returns each layout as a ready-to-run [ISA circuit](/guides/transpile#instruction-set-architecture): payload, check ancillas, and measurements, already placed on physical qubits. Every circuit therefore goes to hardware exactly as scored, with no further transpilation (because re-routing would move the checks off the qubits the search chose). Submit all circuits in one job and wait for completion before proceeding.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapasok ng addon ang mga check nito sa transpiled na circuit, kaya ibinabalik nito ang bawat layout bilang handa-nang-patakbuhin na [ISA circuit](/guides/transpile#instruction-set-architecture): payload, mga check ancilla, at mga measurement, na nakalagay na sa mga pisikal na qubit. Kaya ang bawat circuit ay pumupunta sa hardware nang eksakto kung paano ito na-score, nang walang karagdagang transpilation (dahil ang re-routing ay maglilipat sa mga check palayo sa mga qubit na pinili ng paghahanap). Isumite ang lahat ng circuit sa iisang job at hintayin ang pagkumpleto bago magpatuloy.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The submitted circuits retain their check rules, sampled stabilizers, and model scores in metadata. When retrieving a job, use these saved definitions. Repeating the search can change the checks and must not change how an earlier job is analyzed.\n"
msgstr "Pinapanatili ng mga isinumiteng circuit ang kanilang mga check rule, na-sample na stabilizer, at model score sa metadata. Kapag kumukuha ng job, gamitin ang mga naka-save na depinisyong ito. Ang pag-ulit ng paghahanap ay maaaring magbago ng mga check at hindi dapat magbago kung paano sinusuri ang naunang job.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following explains how the post-selection works. Each circuit measures the data qubits into register `meas` and the check ancillas into register `checks_c`. Each check contributes one syndrome bit per shot, the XOR of the measured bits of the qubits in its `check_support`; `0` means the comparison came out clean and `1` means the check fired. `cc.get_postselection_method()` returns the rule that reads this record. We rebuild it from the check definitions and the register and measurement map saved with each circuit, then pass it the bitstring from `join_data()` in that circuit's register order, and a shot is kept only when no check fired. The value of $G$ in a shot is its sign times the parity of the data bits on its support, as described above.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapaliwanag ng sumusunod kung paano gumagana ang post-selection. Sinusukat ng bawat circuit ang mga data qubit sa register na `meas` at ang mga check ancilla sa register na `checks_c`. Ang bawat check ay nag-aambag ng isang syndrome bit bawat shot, ang XOR ng mga nasukat na bit ng mga qubit sa `check_support` nito; ang `0` ay nangangahulugang malinis ang kinalabasan ng paghahambing at ang `1` ay nangangahulugang tumunog ang check. Ibinabalik ng `cc.get_postselection_method()` ang patakarang bumabasa sa record na ito. Itinatayo naming muli ito mula sa mga depinisyon ng check at sa register at measurement map na naka-save sa bawat circuit, pagkatapos ay ipinapasa rito ang bitstring mula sa `join_data()` sa register order ng circuit na iyon, at ang shot ay pinapanatili lang kapag walang tumunog na check. Ang halaga ng $G$ sa isang shot ay ang sign nito na pinarami sa parity ng mga data bit sa support nito, tulad ng inilarawan sa itaas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The table reports the following metrics for each layout:\n"
msgstr "Iniuulat ng talahanayan ang sumusunod na mga metric para sa bawat layout:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Column | Meaning |\n"
"|:-------|:--------|\n"
"| `layout` | B0 has no checks; P1, P2, P4, and the last P layout hold that many committed checks |\n"
"| `checks` | Number of checks per circuit |\n"
"| `gamma` | The addon's residual-noise cancellation cost estimate from Section 2.5.2 |\n"
"| `added 2Q gates` | Two-qubit gates added to the unchecked circuit |\n"
"| `A` | *Post-selection rate*: fraction of shots passing all checks (1.0 = no checks) |\n"
"| `1/A` | *Sampling overhead*: how many raw shots are needed per accepted shot |\n"
"| `raw` | Mean signed $\\langle G\\rangle$ over all shots, before post-selection |\n"
"| `post` | Mean signed $\\langle G\\rangle$ over the accepted shots only |\n"
"| `raw_ci`, `post_ci` | 95% half-widths from shot noise, for this fixed set of stabilizers |\n"
"| `gain vs B0` | `post` minus the `raw` value of B0 for the same stabilizer |\n"
"| `gain_se` | Standard error of that gain across the sampled stabilizers |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Column | Kahulugan |\n"
"|:-------|:--------|\n"
"| `layout` | Walang check ang B0; ang P1, P2, P4, at ang huling P layout ay may ganoong karaming na-commit na check |\n"
"| `checks` | Bilang ng mga check bawat circuit |\n"
"| `gamma` | Ang tantiya ng addon sa cost ng pagkansela ng natitirang ingay mula sa Seksyon 2.5.2 |\n"
"| `added 2Q gates` | Mga two-qubit gate na idinagdag sa unchecked na circuit |\n"
"| `A` | *Post-selection rate*: bahagi ng mga shot na pumasa sa lahat ng check (1.0 = walang check) |\n"
"| `1/A` | *Sampling overhead*: ilang raw shot ang kailangan bawat tinanggap na shot |\n"
"| `raw` | Mean ng signed na $\\langle G\\rangle$ sa lahat ng shot, bago ang post-selection |\n"
"| `post` | Mean ng signed na $\\langle G\\rangle$ sa mga tinanggap na shot lang |\n"
"| `raw_ci`, `post_ci` | 95% half-width mula sa shot noise, para sa nakapirming set na ito ng mga stabilizer |\n"
"| `gain vs B0` | `post` bawas ang `raw` na halaga ng B0 para sa parehong stabilizer |\n"
"| `gain_se` | Standard error ng gain na iyon sa mga na-sample na stabilizer |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rates and values are averaged over the ten sampled stabilizers; `1/A` uses the averaged `A`.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga rate at halaga ay naka-average sa sampung na-sample na stabilizer; ginagamit ng `1/A` ang naka-average na `A`.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Read the table along the check count, and compare the following two things measured on hardware:\n"
msgstr "Basahin ang talahanayan ayon sa bilang ng check, at ihambing ang sumusunod na dalawang bagay na nasukat sa hardware:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*1. Whether the post-selected value improves, and at what cost in post-selection rate.* Each added check can cover more faults, but it also adds gates, and the search commits the best check it finds for each target in turn, even when that check raises gamma. Neither `post` nor gamma is guaranteed to improve at every step, so compare each change in `post` with the measured `A` and the uncertainty of the gain.\n"
msgstr "*1. Kung bumubuti ba ang post-selected na halaga, at sa anong halaga sa post-selection rate.* Ang bawat idinagdag na check ay maaaring sumaklaw ng mas maraming fault, pero nagdaragdag din ito ng mga gate, at kino-commit ng paghahanap ang pinakamahusay na check na nahanap nito para sa bawat target nang isa-isa, kahit na ang check na iyon ay nagpapataas ng gamma. Hindi garantisadong bubuti ang `post` o ang gamma sa bawat hakbang, kaya ihambing ang bawat pagbabago sa `post` sa nasukat na `A` at sa uncertainty ng gain.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*2. How the raw value changes as checks are added.* The added gates can introduce errors, so compare both `raw` and `post` with the raw value of B0. A gain over the checked layout's own raw value is not enough by itself to establish a benefit over running the bare circuit.\n"
msgstr "*2. Paano nagbabago ang raw na halaga habang nagdaragdag ng mga check.* Ang mga idinagdag na gate ay maaaring magdala ng mga error, kaya ihambing ang `raw` at `post` sa raw na halaga ng B0. Ang gain kumpara sa sariling raw na halaga ng checked na layout ay hindi sapat nang mag-isa para patunayan ang benepisyo kumpara sa pagpapatakbo ng bare circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the saved run, raw means decrease along the check ladder, while all post-selected point estimates exceed B0. The checked circuits therefore start from lower raw values, and the improvement appears after filtering. Compare the paired gains and their uncertainty before judging the benefit of each layout.\n"
msgstr "Sa naka-save na run, bumababa ang mga raw mean sa ladder ng check, habang lahat ng post-selected na point estimate ay lumalampas sa B0. Kaya nagsisimula ang mga checked na circuit sa mas mababang raw na halaga, at lumilitaw ang pagbuti pagkatapos ng pag-filter. Ihambing ang mga paired gain at ang uncertainty ng mga ito bago husgahan ang benepisyo ng bawat layout.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/31ee3295-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/31ee3295-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the left panel, faded bars show the raw mean of $\\langle G\\rangle$, solid bars show the post-selected mean, and the dashed line marks the bare baseline. The right panel shows the trade-off between post-selected mean and acceptance.\n"
msgstr "Sa kaliwang panel, ipinapakita ng mga namumutlang bar ang raw mean ng $\\langle G\\rangle$, ipinapakita ng mga solidong bar ang post-selected mean, at minamarkahan ng putol-putol na linya ang bare baseline. Ipinapakita ng kanang panel ang trade-off sa pagitan ng post-selected mean at acceptance.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the saved run, the post-selected mean rises along the check ladder while acceptance falls. The layout with all committed checks gives the highest post-selected mean and the lowest accepted-shot yield. This ordering agrees with decreasing gamma in the model table, but it does not establish which layout is best for every application.\n"
msgstr "Sa naka-save na run, tumataas ang post-selected mean sa ladder ng check habang bumababa ang acceptance. Ang layout na may lahat ng na-commit na check ang nagbibigay ng pinakamataas na post-selected mean at pinakamababang yield ng tinanggap na shot. Tugma ang pagkakasunod-sunod na ito sa bumababang gamma sa model table, pero hindi nito pinatutunayan kung aling layout ang pinakamahusay para sa bawat application.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Gamma is a model score computed without experimental shots, not a guarantee of hardware performance. Agreement gives a rough check of its ranking for these sampled observables, and another backend or calibration can produce a different ordering. Compare the gain, its uncertainty, acceptance, and added circuit cost when choosing a layout.\n"
msgstr "Ang gamma ay isang model score na kinukwenta nang walang experimental shot, hindi garantiya ng performance sa hardware. Ang pagtutugma ay nagbibigay ng magaspang na pagsusuri sa ranking nito para sa mga na-sample na observable na ito, at ang ibang backend o calibration ay maaaring magbunga ng ibang pagkakasunod-sunod. Ihambing ang gain, ang uncertainty nito, ang acceptance, at ang idinagdag na gastos sa circuit kapag pumipili ng layout.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.5.4 Interpretation {#254-interpretation}"
msgstr "2.5.4 Interpretasyon {#254-interpretation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The saved run illustrates the central trade-off of post-selected QEC: accepting fewer shots can improve agreement with the ideal observable values. Every checked layout improves on its own raw mean, and the all-check layout gives the largest post-selected gain over B0. Every checked raw mean is below B0 in this run, so the improvement over bare appears only after filtering.\n"
msgstr "Inilalarawan ng naka-save na run ang sentral na trade-off ng post-selected QEC: ang pagtanggap ng mas kaunting shot ay maaaring magpabuti ng pagtutugma sa mga ideal na halaga ng observable. Bumubuti ang bawat checked na layout kumpara sa sarili nitong raw mean, at ang all-check na layout ang nagbibigay ng pinakamalaking post-selected gain kumpara sa B0. Mas mababa sa B0 ang bawat checked raw mean sa run na ito, kaya lumilitaw lang ang pagbuti kumpara sa bare pagkatapos ng pag-filter.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is the same principle used in the spacetime-code demonstration cited in the introduction, but that demonstration's tenfold figure concerns effective gate error rates. Our observable-by-observable benchmark measures a different quantity and does not reproduce that numerical claim.\n"
msgstr "Ito ang parehong prinsipyong ginamit sa spacetime-code demonstration na binanggit sa panimula, pero ang sampung-ulit na bilang ng demonstration na iyon ay tungkol sa mga epektibong gate error rate. Ang aming observable-by-observable na benchmark ay sumusukat ng ibang dami at hindi nito inuulit ang numerong claim na iyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Report acceptance $A$ alongside the post-selected mean and the uncertainty of the paired gain. The sampling overhead $1/A$ and added gates determine the cost of obtaining accepted shots; more checks need not improve that trade-off. Both the values and their ordering can change with backend calibration, and the model's gamma ranking need not match the hardware ranking.\n"
msgstr "Iulat ang acceptance na $A$ kasama ang post-selected mean at ang uncertainty ng paired gain. Ang sampling overhead na $1/A$ at ang mga idinagdag na gate ang nagtatakda ng gastos sa pagkuha ng mga tinanggap na shot; ang mas maraming check ay hindi kailangang magpabuti sa trade-off na iyon. Ang mga halaga at ang pagkakasunod-sunod ng mga ito ay maaaring magbago ayon sa backend calibration, at ang gamma ranking ng modelo ay hindi kailangang tumugma sa hardware ranking.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-8}"
msgstr "Suriin ang iyong pag-unawa {#check-your-understanding-8}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two layouts use the same qubit layout. One has no checks (acceptance rate = 1.0); the other has multiple checks (lower acceptance rate). The checked layout shows a higher post-selected value of $\\langle G\\rangle$. Which layout is better?\n"
msgstr "Dalawang layout ang gumagamit ng parehong qubit layout. Ang isa ay walang check (acceptance rate = 1.0); ang isa ay may maraming check (mas mababang acceptance rate). Ang checked na layout ay nagpapakita ng mas mataas na post-selected na halaga ng $\\langle G\\rangle$. Alin ang mas mahusay na layout?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*It depends on the application.*\n"
msgstr "*Depende ito sa application.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "If the goal is accurate individual samples, for example in sampling-based algorithms where each accepted shot is a single-shot output, the checked layout might be preferable. Its accepted shots agree more often with the ideal value of the measured observable, and each kept shot is a sample, not a statistical reconstruction. This single-shot property is what distinguishes error detection from error mitigation. A higher post-selected $\\langle G\\rangle$ alone does not show that each accepted shot comes from a higher-fidelity state, because a check can change the data state without changing the statistics of $G$.\n"
msgstr "Kung ang layunin ay tumpak na mga indibidwal na sample, halimbawa sa mga sampling-based na algorithm kung saan ang bawat tinanggap na shot ay isang single-shot na output, ang checked na layout ay maaaring mas ginusto. Mas madalas na tumutugma ang mga tinanggap nitong shot sa ideal na halaga ng sinusukat na observable, at ang bawat itinagong shot ay isang sample, hindi isang statistical reconstruction. Ang single-shot na katangiang ito ang naghihiwalay sa error detection mula sa error mitigation. Ang mas mataas na post-selected na $\\langle G\\rangle$ lang ay hindi nagpapakita na ang bawat tinanggap na shot ay nagmumula sa state na mas mataas ang fidelity, dahil ang isang check ay maaaring magbago ng data state nang hindi binabago ang statistics ng $G$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "If the goal is many samples quickly and lower per-shot accuracy is acceptable (for example, for estimating expectation values), the unchecked layout may be more practical, because it produces more usable shots from the same batch.\n"
msgstr "Kung ang layunin ay maraming sample nang mabilis at katanggap-tanggap ang mas mababang per-shot accuracy (halimbawa, para sa pagtantiya ng mga expectation value), ang unchecked na layout ay maaaring mas praktikal, dahil mas maraming magagamit na shot ang nabubuo nito mula sa parehong batch.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*A useful shorthand for comparing layouts.* A layout with post-selection rate $A$ keeps about $A N$ of $N$ raw shots, so it needs $1/A$ times as many raw shots for the same number of accepted shots. Equal accepted counts give equal precision only if the observable's variance among the accepted shots is also similar. Set that cost against the gap between the post-selected value and the raw value of the bare layout. Comparing layouts on both numbers illustrates that more checks do not always win.\n"
msgstr "*Isang kapaki-pakinabang na shorthand para sa paghahambing ng mga layout.* Ang layout na may post-selection rate na $A$ ay nagtatago ng humigit-kumulang $A N$ sa $N$ raw shot, kaya kailangan nito ng $1/A$ beses na mas maraming raw shot para sa parehong bilang ng tinanggap na shot. Ang magkakapantay na bilang ng tinanggap ay nagbibigay ng magkakapantay na precision lamang kung magkahawig din ang variance ng observable sa mga tinanggap na shot. Ihambing ang gastos na iyon sa agwat ng post-selected na halaga at ng raw na halaga ng bare na layout. Ang paghahambing ng mga layout sa dalawang numerong ito ay nagpapakita na hindi laging nananalo ang mas maraming check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Caveat: error detection is not unbiased.*\n"
msgstr "*Babala: hindi unbiased ang error detection.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Post-selection removes the errors the checks detect, but not all errors. Faults that commute with every check or that occur outside any check's detection region pass through undetected. The post-selected distribution therefore remains *biased*: post-selection can reduce the bias, but it guarantees neither an improvement nor its complete removal. For tasks that require unbiased estimates of observables, error detection has to be combined with other error mitigation techniques (for example, probabilistic error cancellation on the post-selected data). The three relevant axes are:\n"
msgstr "Inaalis ng post-selection ang mga error na natutukoy ng mga check, pero hindi ang lahat ng error. Ang mga fault na nagko-commute sa bawat check o nangyayari sa labas ng detection region ng anumang check ay dumadaan nang hindi natutukoy. Kaya nananatiling *biased* ang post-selected na distribution: maaaring bawasan ng post-selection ang bias, pero hindi nito ginagarantiya ang pagbuti o ang kumpletong pag-alis nito. Para sa mga gawaing nangangailangan ng unbiased na tantiya ng mga observable, ang error detection ay kailangang pagsamahin sa iba pang error mitigation technique (halimbawa, probabilistic error cancellation sa post-selected na data). Ang tatlong kaugnay na axis ay:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Bias*: detection can reduce it, with no guarantee of a reduction or of its complete removal;\n"
msgstr "*Bias*: maaari itong bawasan ng detection, nang walang garantiya ng pagbabawas o ng kumpletong pag-alis nito;\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Variance*: at comparable per-shot variance, fewer accepted shots mean higher statistical noise per estimate;\n"
msgstr "*Variance*: sa maihahambing na per-shot variance, ang mas kaunting tinanggap na shot ay nangangahulugang mas mataas na statistical noise bawat tantiya;\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Cost*: checks add gates, and rejected shots reduce the usable yield.\n"
msgstr "*Cost*: nagdaragdag ng mga gate ang mga check, at binabawasan ng mga tinanggihang shot ang magagamit na yield.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Design must consider all three of these.\n"
msgstr "Dapat isaalang-alang ng disenyo ang lahat ng tatlong ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Wrap-up {#wrap-up}"
msgstr "Buod {#wrap-up}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson covered error detection in quantum circuits through two complementary approaches that differ in where and how checks are placed. Part 1 focused on end-of-circuit detection (checks applied after the circuit completes), while Part 2 developed mid-circuit detection (checks placed at interior points during circuit execution).\n"
msgstr "Tinalakay ng leksyong ito ang error detection sa mga quantum circuit sa pamamagitan ng dalawang komplementaryong paraan na magkaiba kung saan at paano inilalagay ang mga check. Nakatuon ang Part 1 sa end-of-circuit detection (mga check na inilalapat pagkatapos matapos ang circuit), habang binuo ng Part 2 ang mid-circuit detection (mga check na inilalagay sa mga panloob na punto habang tumatakbo ang circuit).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Part 1: End-of-circuit detection* demonstrated two strategies. Symmetry post-selection ([Section 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection)) uses known output constraints, like GHZ's two-bitstring support, to filter corrupted shots in classical post-processing, requiring no extra qubits but only catching errors visible in the final measurement basis. Stabilizer-code post-selection ([Section 1.2](#stabilizer-code)) adds ancilla qubits and syndrome extraction at the circuit's end, detecting both bit-flip and phase-flip errors through the $[[4,2,2]]$ code. The key limitation: all check information is collected at the final boundary, so the syndrome cannot localize when or where faults occurred during execution.\n"
msgstr "Ipinakita ng *Part 1: End-of-circuit detection* ang dalawang estratehiya. Ang symmetry post-selection ([Seksyon 1.1](#symmetry-post-selection)) ay gumagamit ng mga kilalang output constraint, tulad ng two-bitstring support ng GHZ, para i-filter ang mga sirang shot sa classical post-processing, na hindi nangangailangan ng dagdag na qubit pero mga error lang na nakikita sa huling measurement basis ang nahuhuli. Ang stabilizer-code post-selection ([Seksyon 1.2](#stabilizer-code)) ay nagdaragdag ng mga ancilla qubit at syndrome extraction sa dulo ng circuit, na nakakatukoy ng parehong bit-flip at phase-flip na error sa pamamagitan ng $[[4,2,2]]$ code. Ang pangunahing limitasyon: nalilikom ang lahat ng impormasyon ng check sa huling hangganan, kaya hindi matutukoy ng syndrome kung kailan o saan nangyari ang mga fault habang tumatakbo.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Part 2: Mid-circuit detection* moved checks inside the circuit to access errors as they happen. Throughout, mid-circuit refers to where a check's detection region reaches, the circuit interior; the ancillas themselves are read out at the end alongside the data, with no dynamic-circuit operations. Coherent Pauli checks (Sections [2.2](#coherent)-[2.3](#spacetime)) provided the algebraic foundation: for any Clifford circuit $U$, the relation $L = U^\\dagger R\\, U$ defines valid check pairs, with detection via anti-commutation. The 20-qubit random Clifford payload provides a benchmark for testing whether the detection benefit outweighs the noise from added gates and routing. Spacetime codes (Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment)) refined this by placing lightweight checks where the circuit structure naturally supports them, with no $L$ needed, and tracing each check's *detection region* backward through the circuit. The `qiskit-paulice` addon automates that search and ranks placements by their model cost for residual noise. Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment) use a deeper version of the same circuit construction and compare checks through sampled stabilizers, weighing the post-selected gain of each added check against the acceptance it costs.\n"
msgstr "Inilipat ng *Part 2: Mid-circuit detection* ang mga check sa loob ng circuit para maabot ang mga error habang nangyayari ang mga ito. Sa kabuuan, ang mid-circuit ay tumutukoy sa kung hanggang saan umaabot ang detection region ng check, ang loob ng circuit; ang mga ancilla mismo ay binabasa sa dulo kasama ng data, nang walang dynamic-circuit na operasyon. Ang mga coherent Pauli check (Mga Seksyon [2.2](#coherent)-[2.3](#spacetime)) ay nagbigay ng algebraic na pundasyon: para sa anumang Clifford circuit na $U$, ang relasyong $L = U^\\dagger R\\, U$ ay tumutukoy sa mga wastong check pair, na may detection sa pamamagitan ng anti-commutation. Ang 20-qubit random Clifford payload ay nagbibigay ng benchmark para subukan kung nahihigitan ng benepisyo ng detection ang ingay mula sa mga idinagdag na gate at routing. Pinino ito ng mga spacetime code (Mga Seksyon [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment)) sa pamamagitan ng paglalagay ng magagaan na check kung saan likas na sinusuportahan ng istruktura ng circuit ang mga ito, nang walang kailangang $L$, at pagsubaybay sa *detection region* ng bawat check pabalik sa circuit. Ina-automate ng `qiskit-paulice` addon ang paghahanap na iyon at nire-rank ang mga placement ayon sa model cost nito para sa natitirang ingay. Gumagamit ang Mga Seksyon [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment) ng mas malalim na bersyon ng parehong circuit construction at inihahambing ang mga check sa pamamagitan ng mga na-sample na stabilizer, tinitimbang ang post-selected na gain ng bawat idinagdag na check laban sa acceptance na ibinabayad nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson intentionally does not apply error mitigation or suppression techniques (for example, dynamical decoupling, readout error mitigation, or ZNE) that could further improve the results. The goal is to isolate the effect of error *detection* alone. Martiel & Javadi-Abhari report fidelity gains up to 236$\\times$ in their spacetime-check experiments, which also use dynamical decoupling and readout folding.\n"
msgstr "Sinadya ng leksyong ito na hindi maglapat ng mga error mitigation o suppression technique (halimbawa, dynamical decoupling, readout error mitigation, o ZNE) na maaaring mas magpabuti pa sa mga resulta. Ang layunin ay ihiwalay ang epekto ng error *detection* lamang. Iniulat nina Martiel & Javadi-Abhari ang mga fidelity gain hanggang 236$\\times$ sa kanilang mga spacetime-check na eksperimento, na gumagamit din ng dynamical decoupling at readout folding.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Limitations to consider.*\n"
msgstr "*Mga limitasyong dapat isaalang-alang.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spacetime checks are a *heuristic*. They do not catch all errors. Faults that commute with every check, or that occur outside any detection region, pass undetected. Post-selection can therefore reduce the bias of the results, but it guarantees neither an improvement nor the removal of every error. For unbiased estimation of observables, error detection should be combined with other quantum error mitigation techniques ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. I](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"
msgstr "Ang mga spacetime check ay isang *heuristic*. Hindi nila nahuhuli ang lahat ng error. Ang mga fault na nagko-commute sa bawat check, o nangyayari sa labas ng anumang detection region, ay dumadaan nang hindi natutukoy. Kaya maaaring bawasan ng post-selection ang bias ng mga resulta, pero hindi nito ginagarantiya ang pagbuti o ang pag-alis ng bawat error. Para sa unbiased na pagtantiya ng mga observable, ang error detection ay dapat pagsamahin sa iba pang quantum error mitigation technique ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. I](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The checks are most effective for *Clifford-dominated circuits*; the space of valid checks shrinks exponentially with non-Clifford content ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. V](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"
msgstr "Pinakaepektibo ang mga check para sa mga *Clifford-dominated na circuit*; ang espasyo ng mga wastong check ay lumiliit nang exponential kasabay ng non-Clifford na nilalaman ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. V](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Pauli operator's overall phase becomes a *relative phase between the ancilla branches* when the operator is controlled, and may need compensation by a single-qubit gate on the ancilla wire. The one-sided example of Section 2.3.2 corrects a minus sign on $L$ this way, and general implementations must account for it.\n"
msgstr "Ang kabuuang phase ng isang Pauli operator ay nagiging *relative phase sa pagitan ng mga ancilla branch* kapag kontrolado ang operator, at maaaring mangailangan ng kompensasyon sa pamamagitan ng single-qubit gate sa ancilla wire. Itinatama ng one-sided na halimbawa ng Seksyon 2.3.2 ang minus sign sa $L$ sa ganitong paraan, at dapat itong isaalang-alang ng mga pangkalahatang implementasyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-18
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Three concrete open directions follow from this lesson. First, Sections [2.4](#paulice) and [2.5](#hardware-experiment) apply the placement search to a ten-layer 20-qubit payload; how its runtime scales to deeper circuits on hundreds of qubits determines whether the approach is practical for larger utility-scale circuits. Second, Part 2 used a random Clifford circuit construction at two depths. A natural next step is to check how the detection benefit holds up on deeper circuits and on circuits with some non-Clifford content, such as QAOA or VQE ansatze. Third, spacetime codes here use syndrome information for post-selection rather than active correction, and their heuristic construction has no particular distance guarantee. Moving to fault-tolerant correction requires checks with suitable distance, fault-tolerant syndrome extraction, and a decoder.\n"
msgstr "May tatlong konkretong bukas na direksyon na nagmumula sa araling ito. Una, ang Mga Seksyon [2.4](#paulice) at [2.5](#hardware-experiment) ay naglalapat ng placement search sa isang sampung-layer na 20-qubit na payload; kung paano lumalaki ang runtime nito para sa mas malalalim na circuit sa daan-daang qubit ang magpapasya kung praktikal ang pamamaraan para sa mas malalaking utility-scale na circuit. Ikalawa, gumamit ang Part 2 ng random na Clifford circuit construction sa dalawang depth. Natural na susunod na hakbang ang pagsuri kung paano tumatagal ang benepisyo ng detection sa mas malalalim na circuit at sa mga circuit na may bahagyang non-Clifford na nilalaman, tulad ng mga ansatze ng QAOA o VQE. Ikatlo, gumagamit dito ang mga spacetime code ng syndrome information para sa post-selection sa halip na aktibong correction, at walang partikular na distance guarantee ang kanilang heuristic na konstruksyon. Ang paglipat sa fault-tolerant correction ay nangangailangan ng mga check na may angkop na distance, fault-tolerant na syndrome extraction, at isang decoder.\n"

